// CHEM 225 · chapter "sn2-1" · every passage at the other levels and densities (see ../levels.js for the scheme).
// Key is "level.density"; 5.2 is the passage in chapters.js and is not repeated. Checked by tools/check-levels.js.
(window.READ_LEVELS = window.READ_LEVELS || { text: {} }).text["sn2-1"] = {
  // ---------- Passage 1 · Warm-up: an acid-base reaction is the pattern for everything that follows
  0: {
    "1.1": `<p>A <b>Lewis base</b> is a thing that gives a pair of electrons to make a new bond. It gives the pair to a spot that is a little bit positive.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>Atoms hold on to each other with bonds. A bond is a pair of tiny electrons that two atoms share.</p><p>Hydroxide has a pair of electrons it can give away. It also has a negative charge. A thing that gives away a pair is called a <b>Lewis base</b>.</p><p>Now look at H-Cl. The chlorine pulls the shared electrons toward itself, like the strong side in a tug of war. So the hydrogen is left a little bit positive. A thing that takes in a pair is called a <b>Lewis acid</b>. H-Cl is one.</p><p>Hydroxide gives its pair to the hydrogen, and a new bond forms. At the same time the old bond breaks. Chlorine keeps both of its electrons and walks away as chloride.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Later, the same thing happens at a carbon instead of a hydrogen. That is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>Here is a picture to hold on to. A small piece with extra electrons meets a spot that is short of electrons. The extra electrons move to the short spot. A new bond forms. Chemists use this picture again and again, so it is worth learning well.</p><p>First, some words. Atoms stick together with bonds. A bond is a pair of electrons that two atoms share. Electrons have a negative charge. A pair that is not in a bond, but sits on one atom, is called a lone pair.</p><p>Our first piece is hydroxide. It has lone pairs on its oxygen, and it has a negative charge. A thing that can give a pair of electrons to something else is called a <b>Lewis base</b>. So hydroxide is a Lewis base.</p><p>Our second piece is hydrogen chloride, H-Cl. Hydrogen and chlorine share a bond, but they do not share it fairly. Chlorine pulls the electrons closer to itself, like the strong side in a tug of war. That leaves the hydrogen a little bit positive. A thing that can take in a pair of electrons is called a <b>Lewis acid</b>. The hydrogen of H-Cl is the part that takes in the pair, so H-Cl is a Lewis acid.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Now the two pieces meet. We show what happens with two curved arrows.</p><ul><li>The first arrow starts at a lone pair on the oxygen and ends at the hydrogen. It shows a new O-H bond forming.</li><li>The second arrow starts at the H-Cl bond and ends on the chlorine. It shows the old bond breaking. Chlorine keeps both electrons and leaves as chloride.</li></ul><p>A curved arrow always shows electrons moving. The tail is where the electrons start. The head is where they end up.</p><p>We can check the charges. The oxygen gave up a lone pair to make a bond, so it is no longer negative. The chlorine now owns a whole pair that it used to share, so it is negative. There was one negative charge before. There is one negative charge after. Nothing was lost.</p><p>Why does hydroxide go to the hydrogen and not to the chlorine? The hydrogen is the positive end. A piece with extra electrons goes to the spot that is short of them. Chlorine is also a good one to carry the negative charge once it has left.</p><p>This picture comes back next, with one change. The hydrogen is swapped for a carbon that is also a little bit positive. When the spot being attacked is a carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>. The arrows stay the same. Only the name changes.</p><p>It shows up on exams too. In Question 1D of the past exam, the last step is an oxygen taking a hydrogen from {{H3O+}}. In Question 9, the last step is water pulling off an O-H hydrogen. If you can draw these two arrows without thinking, you keep the easy points at the end of a long answer.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>A <b>Lewis base</b> gives a pair of electrons to a <b>Lewis acid</b>, which takes the pair in. In the reaction of hydroxide with H-Cl, a new bond forms while an old bond breaks.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>Hydroxide meets hydrogen chloride (H-Cl). Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge. It gives a pair of electrons away, so it is the <b>Lewis base</b>.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>In H-Cl, chlorine pulls the shared electrons toward itself. That leaves the hydrogen a little bit positive, written δ<sup>+</sup>. The hydrogen takes in the pair, so H-Cl is the <b>Lewis acid</b>.</p><p>Two curved arrows show the story:</p><ul><li>The first arrow goes from a lone pair on oxygen to the hydrogen. A new O-H bond forms.</li><li>The second goes from the H-Cl bond to chlorine. The bond breaks, and chlorine keeps both electrons. It leaves as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>Remember this picture: an atom with extra electrons attacks an atom that is short of them, and at the same time a bond to a group that can hold a negative charge breaks. Later, the atom being attacked is a carbon instead of a hydrogen. The arrows are the same. Only the name changes. It is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>Chemistry has a pattern that shows up again and again. It is easiest to see in a simple reaction: hydroxide meets hydrogen chloride (H-Cl).</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge. It can give a pair of electrons to another atom. A particle that gives an electron pair is called a <b>Lewis base</b>.</p><p>H-Cl is different. Chlorine pulls the shared electrons toward itself, so the hydrogen ends up a little bit positive, written δ<sup>+</sup>. That hydrogen can take in an electron pair. A particle that takes in an electron pair is called a <b>Lewis acid</b>. So H-Cl is the Lewis acid here.</p><p>We draw the story with two curved arrows. Each arrow moves one pair of electrons. The tail sits on the electrons that move. The head points to where they end up.</p><ul><li>The first arrow starts at a lone pair on oxygen and ends at the hydrogen. A new O-H bond forms.</li><li>The second arrow starts at the H-Cl bond and ends on chlorine. The bond breaks, and chlorine keeps both electrons. It leaves as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>An arrow never starts on a positive charge or on a bare atom that has no electrons to give.</p><p>Now check the charges after the arrows act. Oxygen used a lone pair to make a bond, so its charge goes from negative one to zero. Chlorine now owns a pair it used to share, so its charge goes from zero to negative one. The total charge was negative one before and is negative one after.</p><p>Why does hydroxide attack the hydrogen and not the chlorine? Chlorine pulls electrons harder than hydrogen does, so the hydrogen is the poor end of the bond. A particle with extra electrons goes to the end that is short of them. Chlorine is also the atom that can carry the negative charge afterward.</p><p>Hold on to the whole picture: an atom with extra electrons attacks an atom that is short of them, and at the same time a bond to a group that can carry a negative charge breaks. In the next step the hydrogen is swapped for a carbon that is also a little bit positive. The arrows do not change. Only the name does. When the atom being attacked is carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>This pattern helps on exams. In Question 1D of the past exam, the last step is an oxygen taking a hydrogen from {{H3O+}}, which uses exactly these two arrows. In Question 9, the last step is water pulling off an O-H hydrogen. If these arrows come to you automatically, you can give the harder steps your full attention and you keep the easy points at the end of a long answer.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>Hydroxide is a <b>Lewis base</b>: it gives an electron pair to the partly positive hydrogen of H-Cl, the <b>Lewis acid</b>, while the H-Cl bond breaks. When the atom attacked is carbon, this is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>Take hydroxide and hydrogen chloride (H-Cl). Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge, so it can give away an electron pair. A species that gives an electron pair is a <b>Lewis base</b>.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>In H-Cl, chlorine pulls the bonding electrons toward itself, so the hydrogen is partly positive (δ<sup>+</sup>). That hydrogen can accept the pair, so H-Cl is a <b>Lewis acid</b>, a species that accepts an electron pair.</p><p>Curved arrows show the electrons moving:</p><ul><li>The first arrow starts at a lone pair on oxygen and ends at the hydrogen. A new O-H bond forms.</li><li>The second starts at the H-Cl bond and ends on chlorine. The bond breaks and chlorine keeps both electrons, leaving as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>The pattern to remember: an electron-rich atom attacks an electron-poor atom, and at the same time the bond to a group that can carry a negative charge breaks. Next, the electron-poor hydrogen is replaced by an electron-poor carbon. The arrows stay the same. When the atom being attacked is carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>Many reactions in this course follow one simple pattern, and it is easiest to see in a reaction you already know: hydroxide meets hydrogen chloride (H-Cl).</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge. It can hand an electron pair to another atom. A species that donates an electron pair is a <b>Lewis base</b>.</p><p>In H-Cl, chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen, which means it pulls the bonding electrons toward itself. The hydrogen is left partly positive, written δ<sup>+</sup>. That hydrogen can accept the pair. A species that accepts an electron pair is a <b>Lewis acid</b>, so H-Cl is the Lewis acid.</p><p>Two curved arrows tell the whole story. A curved arrow moves one pair of electrons: its tail sits on the electrons that move (a lone pair or a bond) and its head points to where they end up (a new bond or an atom).</p><ul><li>The first arrow starts at a lone pair on oxygen and ends at the hydrogen. This makes a new O-H bond.</li><li>The second arrow starts at the H-Cl bond and ends on chlorine. The bond breaks and chlorine keeps both electrons, leaving as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>An arrow never starts on a positive charge or on a bare atom with no electrons to give.</p><p>You can check your work with charges. Oxygen used a lone pair to make a bond, so its charge goes from negative one to zero. Chlorine took full ownership of a pair it used to share, so its charge goes from zero to negative one. The total charge before and after is negative one, as it must be.</p><p>Why is the hydrogen the target and not the chlorine? Chlorine is more electronegative, so the hydrogen is the electron-poor end of the bond. An electron-rich species goes to the electron-poor end. Chlorine is also the atom that can carry the negative charge once the bond breaks.</p><p>Keep this picture: an electron-rich atom attacks an electron-poor atom, and the bond to a group that can carry a negative charge breaks at the same time. In the next step the partly positive hydrogen is replaced by a partly positive carbon. The arrows do not change at all. Only the name changes. When the atom being attacked is carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>This matters on the exam. In Question 1D of the past exam, the last step is a carboxylate oxygen taking H from {{H3O+}}, which is exactly these two arrows. In Question 9, the last step is water removing an O-H proton. If these arrows are automatic, the harder steps of a mechanism get your full attention, and you do not lose the easy points at the end of a long answer. A good habit is to draw the arrows first, then write the charges, and only then name the roles of the Lewis acid, the Lewis base and the leaving chloride.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>Hydroxide, the <b>Lewis base</b>, donates a lone pair to the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen of H-Cl, the <b>Lewis acid</b>, as the H-Cl bond breaks. With a carbon in place of the hydrogen, the same two arrows describe a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>Hydroxide reacts with hydrogen chloride. Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge. It donates an electron pair, so it is the <b>Lewis base</b>.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>In H-Cl, chlorine pulls the bonding electrons toward itself, so the hydrogen carries a partial positive charge (δ<sup>+</sup>). That hydrogen accepts the pair, so HCl is the <b>Lewis acid</b>.</p><p>Two curved arrows describe the reaction:</p><ul><li>The first starts at a lone pair on oxygen and ends at the hydrogen: a new O-H bond forms.</li><li>The second starts at the H-Cl bond and ends on chlorine: the bond breaks and chlorine keeps both electrons, leaving as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>Keep the general picture: an electron-rich atom attacks an electron-poor atom while the bond to a group that can carry a negative charge breaks. Replace the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen with a δ<sup>+</sup> carbon and the arrows are identical. Only the name changes: when the attacked atom is carbon, the reaction is a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>The unit on substitution starts from a reaction you already know: hydroxide with hydrogen chloride. Everything that follows uses the same pattern.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge, and it donates an electron pair. That makes it a <b>Lewis base</b>. In H-Cl, chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen and pulls the bonding electrons toward itself, so the hydrogen is δ<sup>+</sup>. That hydrogen accepts the pair, which makes HCl the <b>Lewis acid</b>.</p><p>Two curved arrows tell the whole story. A curved arrow moves one pair of electrons. Its tail sits on the electrons that move, which is a lone pair or a bond. Its head points to where they end up, which is a new bond or an atom.</p><ul><li>First arrow: from a lone pair on oxygen to the hydrogen, forming a new O-H bond.</li><li>Second arrow: from the H-Cl bond to chlorine. The bond breaks, and chlorine keeps both electrons and leaves as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>An arrow never starts on a positive charge or on a bare atom with no electrons to give. Charges give you a check: oxygen used a lone pair to make a bond, so it goes from negative one to zero. Chlorine gained full ownership of a pair it used to share, so it goes from zero to negative one. The total charge, negative one, is the same before and after.</p><p>The hydrogen is the atom attacked because chlorine is more electronegative, so the bonding electrons sit closer to chlorine and the hydrogen is electron-poor. An electron-rich species goes to the electron-poor end of the bond. Chlorine is also the atom that can carry the negative charge afterward.</p><p>This is the picture to remember. An electron-rich atom attacks an electron-poor atom, and the bond to a group that can carry a negative charge breaks at the same time. In the next step the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen is replaced by a δ<sup>+</sup> carbon. The arrows do not change. Only the name changes: when the atom being attacked is carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>You will use this on the exam. In Question 1D of the past exam, the last step is a carboxylate oxygen taking H from {{H3O+}}, which is exactly these two arrows. In Question 9, the last step is water removing an O-H proton. Proton transfers appear inside longer mechanisms all through the unit. If these two arrows are automatic, the harder steps get your full attention and you keep the easy points at the end of a long answer. A good check on any proton transfer you draw is to ask three things: where does the pair start, where does it end, and does the total charge stay the same? If you can answer all three, the step is almost certainly drawn correctly.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>Hydroxide (a <b>Lewis base</b>) attacks the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen of H-Cl (a <b>Lewis acid</b>) while the H-Cl bond breaks and chloride leaves. The same two arrows with a δ<sup>+</sup> carbon in place of hydrogen are a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>Chapter 7 opens with a reaction from Chapter 6: hydroxide meets hydrogen chloride.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge. It gives an electron pair, so it is the <b>Lewis base</b>. In H-Cl the hydrogen is δ<sup>+</sup>, because chlorine pulls the bonding electrons toward itself. That hydrogen accepts the pair, so HCl is the <b>Lewis acid</b>.</p><p>Two curved arrows tell the whole story. A curved arrow moves one pair of electrons: its tail sits on the electrons that move (a lone pair or a bond) and its head points to where they end up (a new bond or an atom). The first arrow starts at a lone pair on oxygen and ends at the hydrogen: a new O-H bond. The second starts at the H-Cl bond and ends on chlorine: the bond breaks and chlorine keeps both electrons, leaving as {{Cl-}}. An arrow never starts on a positive charge or on a bare atom with no electrons to give.</p><p>Check the charges after the arrows act. Oxygen used a lone pair to make a bond, so it goes from -1 to 0. Chlorine gained full ownership of a pair it used to share, so it goes from 0 to -1. The total charge is -1 before and after, which is a quick test that the arrows are drawn correctly.</p><p>The hydrogen is attacked, and not the chlorine, because chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen: the bonding electrons sit closer to chlorine and the hydrogen is δ<sup>+</sup>. An electron-rich species goes to the electron-poor end of the bond, and chlorine is the atom that can carry the negative charge afterward.</p><p>Hold on to this picture. An electron-rich atom attacks an electron-poor atom, and the bond to a group that can carry a negative charge breaks at the same time. In the next step the professor replaces the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen with a δ<sup>+</sup> carbon. The arrows do not change at all. Only the name changes: when the atom being attacked is carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Proton transfers appear inside longer mechanisms all through this unit. In Question 1D of the past exam, the last step is a carboxylate oxygen taking H from {{H3O+}}: exactly these two arrows. In Question 9 the last step is water removing an O-H proton. If these two arrows are automatic, the harder steps of each mechanism get your full attention, and you will not lose the easy points at the end of a long answer. Two habits help. Start every arrow on a lone pair or a bond, never on a charge sign. Finish by adding up the charges on both sides, to confirm that the total is the same before and after.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>Hydroxide, a <b>Lewis base</b>, donates a lone pair into the σ* orbital of the polarized H-Cl bond, the <b>Lewis acid</b> acceptor, and chloride departs with the bonding pair. Placing a polarized carbon in the acceptor role gives a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>The reaction of hydroxide with hydrogen chloride is the template for the whole unit. Hydroxide carries lone pairs and a negative charge and donates an electron pair, so it is the <b>Lewis base</b>. In H-Cl, the greater electronegativity of chlorine polarizes the bond, leaving hydrogen δ<sup>+</sup>. The acceptor is the empty σ* orbital of the H-Cl bond, centered mainly on hydrogen, so HCl is the <b>Lewis acid</b>. Viewed as proton transfer, it is also a Brønsted acid-base reaction.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Two curved arrows encode the electron flow:</p><ul><li>The first runs from an oxygen lone pair to hydrogen, making a new O-H bond.</li><li>The second runs from the H-Cl σ bond to chlorine. The bond breaks heterolytically, and chlorine keeps both electrons and leaves as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>The formal charges account for the result: oxygen goes from -1 to 0 and chlorine from 0 to -1, so the net charge of -1 is conserved. The general pattern is donation from a filled orbital of an electron-rich atom into an empty orbital of an electron-poor atom, with simultaneous heterolysis of a bond to a group able to stabilize a negative charge. Replacing the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen with a δ<sup>+</sup> carbon leaves the arrows unchanged. Only the name changes: when the atom attacked is carbon, the process is a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The acid-base reaction of hydroxide with hydrogen chloride is the model for every mechanism in this unit, and it is worth analysing in the language of electron donation and acceptance.</p>
[[eqr:[OH-] + Cl>>O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has lone pairs and a negative charge. It donates an electron pair, so it is the <b>Lewis base</b>. Its donor orbital is a filled non-bonding orbital on oxygen. In H-Cl the greater electronegativity of chlorine pulls the bonding electrons toward itself and leaves the hydrogen δ<sup>+</sup>. The acceptor orbital is the empty σ* orbital of the H-Cl bond, which has a large coefficient on hydrogen. A species that accepts an electron pair is a <b>Lewis acid</b>, so HCl is the Lewis acid here. Because the acceptor is effectively a proton, the same event is also a Brønsted proton transfer, and the Lewis picture is the more general of the two.</p><p>Curved arrows are a formalism for tracking electron pairs. Each arrow moves one pair: its tail sits on the electrons that move (a lone pair or a bond) and its head points to the atom or bond that receives them.</p><ul><li>The first arrow runs from an oxygen lone pair to hydrogen. A new O-H bond forms.</li><li>The second arrow runs from the H-Cl bond to chlorine. The bond breaks heterolytically, and chlorine keeps both electrons and leaves as {{Cl-}}.</li></ul><p>An arrow cannot start on a positive charge or on a bare atom with no electrons to give, since there is no electron pair there to move. Formal charges are a useful audit. Oxygen donated a lone pair to form a bond, so it goes from -1 to 0. Chlorine took sole ownership of a pair it formerly shared, so it goes from 0 to -1. The net charge of -1 is conserved, as it must be.</p><p>The site of attack follows from polarity and from what can stabilize the departing pair. Chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen, so hydrogen is the electron-poor end of the bond and the target of an electron-rich donor. Chlorine is also the atom able to carry the negative charge once the bond breaks. Attack at chlorine would require it to accept another pair, which has no comparable driving force.</p><p>The general pattern is the point. Electron density flows from a filled orbital on an electron-rich atom into an empty orbital on an electron-poor atom, and in the same step the bond to a group that can bear a negative charge breaks. In the next step the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen is replaced by a δ<sup>+</sup> carbon. The arrows are identical. Only the name changes: when the atom being attacked is carbon, the reaction is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>This also pays off in practice. Proton transfers sit inside longer mechanisms throughout the unit. In Question 1D of the past exam, the last step is a carboxylate oxygen taking H from {{H3O+}}, which is exactly these two arrows. In Question 9, the last step is water removing an O-H proton. If these two arrows are automatic, the harder steps get your full attention, and you will not lose the easy points at the end of a long answer.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 2 · Substitution at carbon: nucleophile, electrophile, leaving group
  1: {
    "1.1": `<p>When a piece with a pair of electrons attacks a carbon and pushes another piece off it, the new piece takes the old piece's place. That is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>Chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon, just as it did from hydrogen. So the carbon in chloromethane is a little bit positive.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has a pair of electrons to give. It attacks the carbon and makes a new bond. At the same moment the bond between carbon and chlorine breaks, and chlorine leaves with both of its electrons.</p><p>Now hydroxide sits where chlorine used to be. One piece has taken the place of another. That is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>A piece that attacks a carbon with a pair of electrons is called a <b>nucleophile</b>. The piece that leaves with the pair is called the <b>leaving group</b>.</p><p>So there are two names to learn. The piece that attacks is the nucleophile. The piece that leaves is the leaving group. Every substitution has both.</p><p>There is one more thing to know. The three hydrogens on the carbon flip over to the other side as the reaction happens. It is like an umbrella that turns inside out in the wind.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>In the last passage, hydroxide attacked a hydrogen. Now we swap that hydrogen for a carbon. The molecule is chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. It is one carbon with three hydrogens and one chlorine.</p><p>Chlorine pulls electrons toward itself. It did this to hydrogen, and it does it to carbon too. So the carbon is a little bit positive.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>Hydroxide has a pair of electrons it can give, and it has a negative charge. Here is what happens, one part at a time.</p><ul><li>A pair of electrons on the oxygen of hydroxide reaches over to the carbon. A new bond forms between oxygen and carbon.</li><li>At the same moment the bond between carbon and chlorine breaks. Both of its electrons go with the chlorine, and chlorine floats off as chloride.</li></ul><p>These are the same two arrows as before. Now hydroxide is on the carbon, in the place where chlorine used to be. One piece took the place of another. That is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>We need names for the three parts. Learn them well, because you will use them in every answer from now on.</p><ul><li>A piece with a pair of electrons to give is a Lewis base. When it attacks a carbon, it gets a new name: <b>nucleophile</b>. Hydroxide is one.</li><li>The molecule that takes the pair in at carbon is called an <b>electrophile</b>. Chloromethane is one.</li><li>The piece that leaves, carrying the pair from its old bond, is called the <b>leaving group</b>. Here it is chlorine.</li></ul><p>Why must something leave? A carbon like this one already has four bonds, and it is full. It has no empty space for a fifth. So the new bond can only form if an old bond lets go. This is different from a carbon that has a double bond to oxygen, as in carbon dioxide. There, the incoming pair can push a pair of the double bond onto the oxygen. In chloromethane the only electrons that can get out of the way are the ones in the bond to the leaving group.</p><p>Here is a common mistake. Some students draw the arrow starting from the carbon, or from the minus sign. Do not do that. Electrons move from the nucleophile to the electrophile. The first arrow starts on a pair of electrons on the nucleophile and ends on the carbon. The second arrow starts on the bond between carbon and the leaving group and ends on the leaving group.</p><p>There is one more fact in the notes. As the reaction happens, the three hydrogens on the carbon flip over to the other side. It is like an umbrella that turns inside out in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>. It is one of the most important facts about this reaction.</p><p>Your own body does this kind of reaction all the time. A helper molecule called SAM carries a {{CH3}} group on a sulfur that has a positive charge. A nitrogen on DNA, or on norepinephrine, attacks the carbon of that group. The sulfur piece leaves. An enzyme holds the pieces in line so that it can happen.</p><p>Last, a question. Why does chloride not push hydroxide off methanol, which would run the reaction backward? Chloride is calm and stable on its own, so it is happy to leave. Hydroxide is much less stable on its own, so it does not leave easily. The reaction goes forward and not back.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>When a <b>nucleophile</b> attacks a carbon and a <b>leaving group</b> departs with its pair of electrons, one group takes the place of another. This is a <b>substitution</b>.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>Now swap H-Cl for chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. Chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon, so the carbon is a little bit positive, just as the hydrogen was.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The two arrows are the same. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and makes a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks, and both of its electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon. That is a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three words to learn:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is a Lewis base. When a Lewis base attacks a carbon, it is called a <b>nucleophile</b>.</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} takes in the pair at carbon. It is called an <b>electrophile</b>.</li><li>Chlorine is the <b>leaving group</b>. It is the group that leaves with the pair of electrons from its old bond.</li></ul><p>Every substitution has all three: one species that attacks, one that is attacked, and one group that leaves.</p><p>One more fact. The three hydrogens on the carbon flip to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>In the last passage, hydroxide attacked a hydrogen. Now swap H-Cl for chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. Chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon just as it did from hydrogen, so the carbon is a little bit positive.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The arrows are the same two. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and makes a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks, and both of its electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon. That is called a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>You will use three new words in every answer from here on:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base. When a Lewis base attacks a carbon, it is called a <b>nucleophile</b>.</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} takes in the pair at carbon. A particle that does this is called an <b>electrophile</b>.</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b>: the group that departs with the pair of electrons from its old bond.</li></ul><p>Why must something leave? Carbon in {{CH3Cl}} already has four bonds and eight valence electrons. It has no empty orbital for a fifth pair. So it cannot keep both the new bond and the old one. Something has to go. A carbon with a double bond to oxygen, like the carbon in {{CO2}}, is different, because the incoming pair can push a pair of the double bond onto oxygen. On a carbon with only single bonds ({{sp3}}) the only electrons that can move out of the way are those of the bond to the leaving group.</p><p>Many students make one mistake: they draw the arrow from the carbon to the oxygen, or from the negative sign instead of from a lone pair. Electrons flow from the nucleophile to the electrophile, never the other way. Start the first arrow on a lone pair of the nucleophile and end it on the carbon. Start the second on the C-Cl bond and end it on the leaving group.</p><p>The notes add one more fact. The three hydrogens on the carbon flip over to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>, and it is one of the defining facts of this reaction.</p><p>Your body runs this reaction all the time. A helper molecule called S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) carries a {{CH3}} group on a sulfur with a positive charge. A nitrogen on DNA, or on norepinephrine, attacks that carbon, and the neutral sulfur compound leaves. An enzyme holds the partners in line. The lecture's sulfonium example has the same leaving group.</p><p>Put the names on the equation. Hydroxide and chloromethane react to give methanol, {{CH3OH}}, and chloride, {{Cl-}}. Hydroxide is the nucleophile, because it brings the pair of electrons. Chloromethane is the electrophile, because it takes the pair in at carbon. Chloride is the leaving group, because it leaves with the old bonding pair. If you can label all three on any equation, you can follow every substitution that comes next.</p><p>One question was left as homework: why does chloride not displace hydroxide from methanol, the reverse reaction? Chloride is a very weak base, so it is stable alone and is a good leaving group. Hydroxide is a strong base, so it is a poor leaving group. The reaction runs toward the weaker base, so it goes forward and not back.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>When a <b>nucleophile</b> attacks a carbon and the <b>leaving group</b> leaves with its pair of electrons, one group replaces another: a <b>substitution</b>. The attacked molecule is the <b>electrophile</b>, and the carbon's groups flip over, called <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>Now swap H-Cl for chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. Chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon just as it did from hydrogen, so the carbon is partly positive (δ<sup>+</sup>).</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The arrows are the same two. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and makes a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks and both electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide takes the place of chloride on carbon: a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three new words, and you will use them in every answer from here on:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base, but a Lewis base that attacks carbon is called a <b>nucleophile</b>.</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid. A species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is called an <b>electrophile</b>.</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b>: the group that departs with the pair of electrons from its old bond.</li></ul><p>One more fact from the notes: the three hydrogens on the carbon flip over to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>, and it is one of the defining facts of this reaction.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>In the last passage, hydroxide attacked a hydrogen. Now swap H-Cl for chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. Chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon just as it did from hydrogen, so the carbon is partly positive (δ<sup>+</sup>).</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The arrows are the same two. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and makes a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks, and both of its electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon. That is a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three new words, and you will use them in every answer from here on:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base, but when a Lewis base attacks carbon it is called a <b>nucleophile</b> (Nu:<sup>-</sup>).</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid. A species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is called an <b>electrophile</b> ({{E+}}).</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b> (LG): the group that departs with the pair of electrons from its old bond.</li></ul><p>Why must something leave? Carbon in {{CH3Cl}} already has four bonds and eight valence electrons. It has no empty orbital to accept a fifth pair, so it cannot keep both the new bond and the old one. This is different from the carbon of {{CO2}}, where the incoming pair can push a pi pair onto oxygen. On a saturated ({{sp3}}) carbon, the only electrons that can move out of the way are those of the sigma bond to the leaving group.</p><p>Watch out for a common mistake. Students often draw the arrow from the carbon to the oxygen, or from the negative sign rather than from a lone pair. Electrons flow from the nucleophile to the electrophile, never the other way. Start the first arrow on a lone pair of the nucleophile and finish it on the carbon. Start the second on the C-LG bond and finish it on the leaving group atom.</p><p>The notes add one more observation. The three hydrogens on carbon flip over to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>, and it turns out to be one of the defining facts of this reaction.</p><p>Your body runs this reaction constantly. The helper molecule S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) carries a {{CH3}} group attached to a positively charged sulfur. A nucleophile such as a nitrogen on DNA, or on the neurotransmitter precursor norepinephrine, attacks that methyl carbon, and the neutral sulfur compound leaves. An enzyme holds the partners in line. The lecture's own sulfonium example has the same leaving group.</p><p>Put the names on the equation. Hydroxide and chloromethane react to give methanol, {{CH3OH}}, and chloride, {{Cl-}}. Hydroxide is the nucleophile because it brings the electron pair. Chloromethane is the electrophile because it accepts the pair at carbon. Chloride is the leaving group because it departs with the old bonding pair. If you can label all three on any equation, every substitution that follows will be easier to read.</p><p>Finally, the professor's homework: why does chloride not displace hydroxide from methanol, the reverse reaction? Chloride is a very weak base, because its conjugate acid HCl is a strong acid. That makes it stable on its own and a good leaving group. Hydroxide is a strong base, because its conjugate acid, water, has {{pKa}} 15.7, so it is a poor leaving group. The equilibrium sits on the side of the weaker base, so the reaction runs forward and not back.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>When a <b>nucleophile</b> attacks the δ<sup>+</sup> carbon of an <b>electrophile</b> and the <b>leaving group</b> departs with the bonding pair, the nucleophile takes its place: a <b>substitution</b>. The carbon's other three groups flip over in <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>Replace H-Cl with chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. Chlorine pulls electrons from carbon as it did from hydrogen, so the carbon is δ<sup>+</sup>.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The two arrows are unchanged. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and forms a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks, and both electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon: a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three terms to use in every answer:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base, but when a Lewis base attacks carbon it is called a <b>nucleophile</b> (Nu:<sup>-</sup>).</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid. A species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is an <b>electrophile</b> ({{E+}}).</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b> (LG): the group that departs with the electron pair from its old bond.</li></ul><p>The notes add one observation. The three hydrogens on carbon flip to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is <b>inversion</b>, and it is one of the defining facts of this reaction.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>Take the pattern from the warm-up and put a carbon where the hydrogen was. In chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}, chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon just as it did from hydrogen, so the carbon is δ<sup>+</sup>.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The arrows are the same two. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and forms a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks and both of its electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon: a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three terms you will use in every answer from here on:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base, but a Lewis base that attacks carbon is called a <b>nucleophile</b> (Nu:<sup>-</sup>).</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid. A species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is an <b>electrophile</b> ({{E+}}).</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b> (LG): the group that departs with the electron pair from its old bond.</li></ul><p>Why must something leave? Carbon in {{CH3Cl}} has four bonds and eight valence electrons, and no empty orbital to take a fifth pair. It cannot hold both the new bond and the old one. Compare the carbon of {{CO2}}, where the incoming pair can push a pi pair onto oxygen. On a saturated ({{sp3}}) carbon, the only electrons that can move aside are those of the sigma bond to the leaving group.</p><p>A common mistake is to draw the arrow from the carbon to the oxygen, or from the negative sign instead of from a lone pair. Electrons flow from the nucleophile to the electrophile. The first arrow starts on a lone pair of the nucleophile and ends on the carbon. The second starts on the C-LG bond and ends on the leaving group atom.</p><p>The notes add one more observation: the three hydrogens on carbon flip over to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>, and it is one of the defining facts of this reaction.</p><p>The same reaction runs in your cells. S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) carries a {{CH3}} group on a positively charged sulfur. A nucleophile such as a nitrogen on DNA, or on the neurotransmitter precursor norepinephrine, attacks that methyl carbon, and the neutral sulfur compound leaves. It is an {{SN2}} reaction on a methyl group, with an enzyme holding the partners in line. The lecture's sulfonium example has the same leaving group.</p><p>Labeling an equation shows how the terms fit. Hydroxide and chloromethane give methanol, {{CH3OH}}, and chloride, {{Cl-}}. Hydroxide brings the electron pair, so it is the nucleophile. Chloromethane accepts the pair at carbon, so it is the electrophile. Chloride departs with the old bonding pair, so it is the leaving group. Every substitution that follows can be read in the same three labels, so it pays to practice finding them before you move on.</p><p>The professor's homework: why does chloride not displace hydroxide from methanol, the reverse reaction? Chloride is a very weak base (its conjugate acid HCl is a strong acid), so it is stable on its own and a good leaving group. Hydroxide is a strong base (its conjugate acid, water, has {{pKa}} 15.7), so it is a poor leaving group. The equilibrium favors the weaker base, so the reaction goes forward, not back.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>A <b>nucleophile</b> (here {{HO-}}) attacks the δ<sup>+</sup> carbon of an <b>electrophile</b> ({{CH3Cl}}) while the <b>leaving group</b> (Cl) departs with its bonding pair: a <b>substitution</b>, with <b>inversion</b> of the carbon's three other groups.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>Now swap H-Cl for chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}. Chlorine pulls electrons away from carbon just as it did from hydrogen, so the carbon is δ<sup>+</sup>.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The arrows are the same two. A lone pair on oxygen attacks the carbon and makes a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks and both of its electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon: a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three new words, and you will use them in every answer from here on:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base, but when a Lewis base attacks carbon it is called a <b>nucleophile</b> (Nu:<sup>-</sup>).</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid. A species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is called an <b>electrophile</b> ({{E+}}).</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b> (LG): the group that departs with the pair of electrons from its old bond.</li></ul><p>Carbon in {{CH3Cl}} already has four bonds and eight valence electrons. It has no empty orbital to accept a fifth pair, so it cannot keep both the new bond and the old one. Something must leave. That is the difference between this carbon and the carbon of {{CO2}}, where the incoming pair can push a π pair onto oxygen. On a saturated ({{sp3}}) carbon the only electrons that can move out of the way are those of a σ bond to the leaving group.</p><p>A common mistake is to draw the arrow from the carbon to the oxygen, or from the negative sign rather than from a lone pair. Electrons flow from the nucleophile to the electrophile, never the other way. Start the first arrow on a lone pair of the nucleophile and finish it on the carbon. Start the second on the C-LG bond and finish it on the leaving group atom.</p><p>The notes add one more observation. The three hydrogens on carbon flip over to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>, and it turns out to be one of the defining facts of this reaction.</p><p>The reaction runs constantly in the body. The cofactor S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) carries a {{CH3}} group attached to a positively charged sulfur. A nucleophile such as a nitrogen on DNA or on the neurotransmitter precursor norepinephrine attacks that methyl carbon, and the neutral sulfur compound leaves. It is an {{SN2}} reaction on a methyl group, with an enzyme holding the partners in line. The lecture's own sulfonium example on 11/05 page 4 has the same leaving group.</p><p>To label an equation, ask three questions. Which species brings the electron pair to carbon? That is the nucleophile, here {{HO-}}. Which species accepts it at carbon? That is the electrophile, here {{CH3Cl}}. Which group leaves with the old bonding pair? That is the leaving group, here chloride. The products are methanol, {{CH3OH}}, and {{Cl-}}.</p><p>The professor's homework asks why the reverse reaction, chloride displacing hydroxide from methanol, does not occur. Chloride is a very weak base (its conjugate acid HCl is a strong acid), so it is stable on its own and is a good leaving group. Hydroxide is a strong base (its conjugate acid, water, has {{pKa}} 15.7), so it is a poor leaving group. The equilibrium sits on the side of the weaker base, so the reaction runs forward and not back.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>A <b>nucleophile</b> donates a pair into the σ* orbital of the C-LG bond of an <b>electrophile</b>, displacing the <b>leaving group</b> with its bonding pair: an {{SN2}} <b>substitution</b> at {{sp3}} carbon, which proceeds with <b>inversion</b> of configuration.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>Replacing the δ<sup>+</sup> hydrogen of H-Cl with the δ<sup>+</sup> carbon of chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}, keeps the same two arrows. A lone pair on oxygen attacks carbon and forms a C-O bond, while the C-Cl bond breaks heterolytically and both electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has replaced chloride on carbon: a <b>substitution</b>.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>Three terms are used throughout:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} remains a Lewis base, but a Lewis base that attacks carbon is a <b>nucleophile</b> (Nu:<sup>-</sup>).</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid: a species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is an <b>electrophile</b> ({{E+}}).</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b> (LG), the group that departs with the electron pair of its former bond.</li></ul><p>Carbon in {{CH3Cl}} has four bonds and a filled octet, with no low-lying empty orbital to take a fifth pair. The only acceptor available is the σ* orbital of the C-LG bond, so the new bond can form only as the old one breaks. The three hydrogens flip through the plane they span, like an umbrella turning in the wind. This is <b>inversion</b>, one of the defining observations for this reaction.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>Take the electron-pushing pattern of the warm-up and place carbon where hydrogen was. In chloromethane, {{H3C-Cl}}, chlorine is more electronegative than carbon and pulls the bonding electrons toward itself, as it did in H-Cl, so the carbon is δ<sup>+</sup>.</p>
[[rxn:[OH-] + CCl>>CO + [Cl-]]]
<p>The two arrows are unchanged. A lone pair on oxygen attacks carbon and forms a C-O bond. The C-Cl bond breaks heterolytically, and both of its electrons leave with chlorine. Hydroxide has taken the place of chloride on carbon: a <b>substitution</b>.</p><p>Three terms carry the vocabulary of the whole unit:</p><ul><li>{{HO-}} is still a Lewis base, but a Lewis base that attacks carbon is called a <b>nucleophile</b> (Nu:<sup>-</sup>).</li><li>{{CH3Cl}} is the Lewis acid. A species that accepts an electron pair at carbon is an <b>electrophile</b> ({{E+}}).</li><li>Cl is the <b>leaving group</b> (LG): the group that departs with the electron pair of its old bond.</li></ul><p>Why the carbon cannot simply add hydroxide: carbon in {{CH3Cl}} has four bonds and eight valence electrons, and it has no low-lying empty orbital to accept a fifth pair. It cannot keep both the new bond and the old one, so something must leave. Contrast the carbon of {{CO2}}, where the incoming pair can push a π pair onto oxygen. On a saturated ({{sp3}}) carbon the only electrons that can move out of the way are those of the σ bond to the leaving group. In orbital terms, the acceptor is the σ* orbital of the C-LG bond.</p><p>A common error is to draw the arrow from the wrong end: from the carbon to the oxygen, or from the negative sign rather than from a lone pair. Electrons flow from the nucleophile to the electrophile. The first arrow starts on a lone pair of the nucleophile and ends on carbon. The second starts on the C-LG bond and ends on the leaving group atom.</p><p>The notes add one more observation. The three hydrogens on carbon flip to the other side as the reaction happens, like an umbrella in the wind. This is <b>inversion</b> of configuration at carbon, and it is one of the defining facts of this reaction. It will be developed in the next passage with a stereocentre.</p><p>Biology uses the same chemistry. The cofactor S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) carries a {{CH3}} group attached to a positively charged sulfur. A nucleophile such as a nitrogen on DNA or on the neurotransmitter precursor norepinephrine attacks that methyl carbon, and the neutral sulfur compound leaves. This is an {{SN2}} reaction at a methyl group, with the enzyme holding the partners in line. The lecture's sulfonium example on 11/05 page 4 has the same leaving group.</p><p>Labelling the equation: the nucleophile is the species that brings the pair to carbon ({{HO-}}), the electrophile is the species that accepts it at carbon ({{CH3Cl}}), and the leaving group is the fragment that departs with the old bonding pair (chloride). The products are methanol, {{CH3OH}}, and {{Cl-}}. The leaving group is by definition the fragment that carries off the electrons of the broken bond, which is why it must be able to stabilize them.</p><p>The professor's homework asks why the reverse reaction, chloride displacing hydroxide from methanol, does not occur. Leaving-group ability tracks the weakness of the base. Chloride is a very weak base, because its conjugate acid HCl is a strong acid, so it is stable on its own and is a good leaving group. Hydroxide is a strong base, because its conjugate acid, water, has {{pKa}} 15.7, so it is a poor leaving group. The equilibrium sits on the side of the weaker base, so the reaction runs forward and not back.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 3 · A stereocentre proves it: one enantiomer gives one enantiomer
  2: {
    "1.1": `<p>The new piece comes in from the <b>back</b>, on the side opposite the piece that leaves. So the shape around the carbon flips, a bit like a left hand turning into a right hand.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>Some molecules come in two shapes that are mirror images, like your left hand and your right hand. They are called enantiomers.</p><p>A carbon can be left-handed or right-handed when it has four different things on it. In the lecture the four things are chlorine, a {{CH3}} group, a hydrogen, and a heavy hydrogen called D. We call the two shapes (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>).</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with only the (<i>R</i>) form. Hydroxide takes the place of chlorine. What comes out is only the (<i>S</i>) form. It is not a mix of both, and it is not the (<i>R</i>) form again.</p><p>The notes say it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>That tells us how hydroxide came in. It did not come in on the same side that chlorine left from. It came in from the <b>back</b>, the side opposite the chlorine. The other three groups swing over like an umbrella in the wind to make room.</p><p>Be careful with the letters. The shape flips, but the letter (<i>R</i> or <i>S</i>) comes from a ranking of the groups, so the letter does not always change. To find the product, first put the new group on the opposite side. Then work out the letter again.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>In chloromethane, {{CH3Cl}}, the carbon has three hydrogens. They all look the same before the reaction and after it. So we cannot see them flip. We need a trick.</p><p>The trick is to use a carbon that has four different things on it. In the lecture, the four are chlorine, a {{CH3}} group, a hydrogen, and a D. D is deuterium. It is a hydrogen that is heavier than normal. It acts like a hydrogen, but it counts as a different thing. A carbon with four different things on it is called a stereocentre.</p><p>A stereocentre can have two shapes. They are mirror images of each other, like your left hand and your right hand. You cannot turn one hand into the other by turning it around. These two shapes are called enantiomers. We label them (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>).</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Now the experiment. Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) form of the chloride. Add hydroxide. The product is the alcohol, and it is the pure (<i>S</i>) form. It is not a mix of both. It is not the (<i>R</i>) form. The notes say it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>What does this tell us? Think about two ways hydroxide could arrive.</p><ul><li>If it came in on the same side that chlorine left from, the other three groups would not need to move. The product would have the same arrangement in space as before.</li><li>That is not what happens. The new OH ends up on the opposite side from where the Cl was. The other three groups swing through to make room, like an umbrella that turns inside out.</li></ul><p>So hydroxide must hit the carbon from the <b>back</b>, straight across from the leaving group.</p><p>A careful point about names. Inversion tells us about the shape: the new group sits on the opposite face. The letters (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>) come from a ranking of the groups. In this reaction chlorine ranks first before, and OH ranks first after. The other three keep their order (C, then D, then H). So the letter flips from (<i>R</i>) to (<i>S</i>). If the new group ranked differently from the old one, the letter could stay the same even though the shape flipped. On an exam, first draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite side, so a wedge becomes a dash. Then work out the letter from the beginning.</p><p>This idea is checked on exams. One question on the past exam shows (<i>S</i>)-2-chloropentane reacting with sodium acetate in DMSO. The acetate ends up on the opposite face. Another question asks which statements do not describe {{SN1}}. The first answer is inversion, because inversion belongs to {{SN2}}.</p><p>Shape matters in medicine, too. The two enantiomers of a drug are different shapes, and the enzyme or receptor in the body usually fits only one of them.</p><p>One last question: why use deuterium? A carbon needs four different groups to be a stereocentre. The carbon of {{CH3CH2Cl}} has two hydrogens that are the same. Swapping one for D makes all four different, and the reaction does not change in any way that matters. So the flip shows up as a change from one enantiomer to the other.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>With a stereocentre, one enantiomer of the starting material gives one enantiomer of the product, with the nucleophile on the opposite face. The nucleophile must attack from the <b>back</b>.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} you cannot see the flip, because the three hydrogens look the same before and after. So the notes use a carbon with four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D (deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen). A carbon like this is a stereocentre. It comes in two mirror-image forms called enantiomers, labeled (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>).</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol. It is not a mixture, and it is not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>This tells us where the nucleophile came from. If hydroxide had attached on the same side that chloride left from, the other three groups would stay where they were, and the arrangement in space would be the same as before. Instead the OH ends up on the opposite side from the Cl, and the other three groups have swung through to make room. So the nucleophile must attack the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>To draw a product on an exam, move the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then work out the letter again from the beginning. The letter can stay the same if the new group ranks differently from the old one.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} you cannot see the flip, because the three hydrogens look the same before and after. So the notes use a carbon that carries four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D. D is deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen. A carbon like this is called a stereocentre.</p><p>A stereocentre comes in two shapes that are mirror images, like a left hand and a right hand. They are called enantiomers, and they are labeled (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>).</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride and add hydroxide. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol. It is not a mixture, and it is not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>This result tells you where the nucleophile came from. If hydroxide had attached on the same side that chloride left from, the other three groups would stay where they were, and the product would have the same arrangement in space as the starting material. Instead the OH ends up on the opposite side from where Cl was, and the three other groups have swung through to make room. The nucleophile must attack the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>Be careful with the letters. Inversion describes the shape: the new group sits on the opposite face. The letters (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>) come from priority rules. Here Cl is the top priority before and OH is the top priority after, and the other three keep their order (C, then D, then H), so the letter flips from (<i>R</i>) to (<i>S</i>). If the nucleophile had a different priority from the leaving group, the letter could stay the same even though the carbon inverted. On an exam, draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then assign the letter fresh.</p><p>You will be asked about this. In one question on the past exam, (<i>S</i>)-2-chloropentane reacts with sodium acetate in DMSO, and the product is drawn with the acetate on the opposite face. Another question asks which statements do not describe {{SN1}}, and the first choice is inversion, because inversion belongs to {{SN2}}. Shape matters in medicine for the same reason: the two enantiomers of a drug are different shapes, and an enzyme or receptor usually fits only one of them.</p><p>Why does deuterium make the experiment possible? A carbon needs four different groups to be a stereocentre, and the carbon of {{CH3CH2Cl}} has two identical hydrogens. Replacing one with D makes the four groups different without changing the chemistry in any way that matters. The flip then shows up as a change from one enantiomer to the other.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>With a stereocentre, <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>. The nucleophile attacks from the <b>back</b>, opposite the leaving group, so the new group ends up on the opposite face.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} you cannot see the flip, because the three hydrogens look the same before and after. So the notes use a carbon that carries four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D (deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen). That carbon is a stereocentre, and it exists as two mirror-image forms called enantiomers, (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>).</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol. Not a mixture, and not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>That result tells you where the nucleophile came from. If hydroxide had attached on the same side that chloride left from, the other three groups would stay where they were and the product would have the same arrangement in space as the starting material. Instead the OH ends up opposite where Cl was, and the other three groups swing through to make room. So the nucleophile attacks the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>Inversion describes the shape, not the letter. To draw a product, move the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then assign (<i>R</i>) or (<i>S</i>) fresh. If the nucleophile ranks differently from the leaving group in the priority rules, the letter can stay the same even though the carbon inverted.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} you cannot see the flip, because the three hydrogens look the same before and after. So the notes use a carbon that carries four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D (deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen). That carbon is a stereocentre. It exists as two mirror-image forms called enantiomers, which are labeled (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>), like a left hand and a right hand.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol. Not a mixture, and not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>That result tells you where the nucleophile came from. If hydroxide had attached on the same side that chloride left from, the three other groups would stay where they were and the product would have the same arrangement in space as the starting material. Instead the OH ends up on the opposite side from where Cl was, and the other three groups have swung through to make room. The nucleophile must attack the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>Inversion of the shape is not always a change of letter. Inversion describes the geometry: the new group sits on the opposite face. The letters (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>) come from priority rules. Here Cl is the top priority group before and OH is the top priority group after, and the other three keep their order (C, then D, then H), so the letter does flip from (<i>R</i>) to (<i>S</i>). If the nucleophile had a different priority rank from the leaving group, the letter could stay the same even though the carbon inverted. On an exam, draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then assign the letter fresh.</p><p>Here is a short way to use all this. Say a question gives you one enantiomer of a chloride and asks for the product with hydroxide. First, put the OH on the face opposite the chlorine. Second, keep the other three groups in the same order but let them swing through. Third, work out the letter again. This gives one pure enantiomer, not a mixture.</p><p>The exam checks this. Question 2A of the past exam shows (<i>S</i>)-2-chloropentane reacting with sodium acetate in DMSO, and the product is drawn with the acetate on the opposite face. Question 3A asks which statements do not describe {{SN1}}, and the first choice is inversion, because inversion belongs to {{SN2}}. Stereochemistry matters in medicine for the same reason: the two enantiomers of a drug are different shapes, and an enzyme or receptor usually fits only one of them.</p><p>Why does deuterium make this experiment possible? A carbon needs four different groups to be a stereocentre, and {{CH3CH2Cl}} has two identical hydrogens. Replacing one H with D makes the four groups different without changing the chemistry in any way that matters, so the flip becomes visible as a change from one enantiomer to the other.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>A pure enantiomer of the substrate gives a pure enantiomer of the product with the nucleophile on the opposite face: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>. The nucleophile attacks from the <b>back</b>, opposite the leaving group.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} the flip cannot be seen, because the three hydrogens look the same before and after. So the notes use a carbon with four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D (deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen). That carbon is a stereocentre.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol: not a mixture, and not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>This shows where the nucleophile came from. Had hydroxide attached on the same side that chloride left from, the other three groups would stay put and the product would have the same arrangement in space as the starting material. Instead the OH ends up opposite the position Cl occupied, and the other three groups swing through to make room. The nucleophile must attack from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>Inversion is a statement about geometry, not about letters. Draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then assign (<i>R</i>) or (<i>S</i>) fresh from the priority rules. The letter changes here because OH ranks first after, as Cl did before. If the nucleophile ranks differently from the leaving group, the letter can stay the same even though the carbon inverted.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} the flip cannot be seen, because the three hydrogens look identical before and after. So the notes use a carbon that carries four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D (deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen). That carbon is a stereocentre, so it comes as a pair of enantiomers, (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>).</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol. Not a mixture, and not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>That result shows where the nucleophile came from. If hydroxide had attached on the same side that chloride left from, the other three groups would stay where they were and the product would have the same spatial arrangement as the starting material. Instead the OH ends up on the opposite side from where Cl was, and the other three groups swing through to make room. The nucleophile must attack the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>Inversion of the shape is not always a change of letter. Inversion describes the geometry: the new group sits on the opposite face. The letters (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>) come from priority rules. Here Cl is the top priority group before and OH is the top priority group after, and the other three keep their order (C, then D, then H), so the letter flips from (<i>R</i>) to (<i>S</i>). If the nucleophile had a different priority rank from the leaving group, the letter could stay the same even though the carbon inverted. On an exam, draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then assign the letter fresh.</p><p>As a procedure: given one enantiomer of a chloride and a nucleophile, put the nucleophile on the face opposite the chlorine, let the other three groups swing through, then assign the letter afresh. The answer is one pure enantiomer, not a mixture.</p><p>Both parts of this appear on the past exam. Question 2A shows (<i>S</i>)-2-chloropentane reacting with sodium acetate in DMSO, and the product is drawn with the acetate on the opposite face. Question 3A asks which statements do not describe {{SN1}}, and the first choice is inversion, because inversion belongs to {{SN2}}. Stereochemistry matters in medicine for the same reason it matters here: the two enantiomers of a drug are different shapes, and an enzyme or receptor usually fits only one of them.</p><p>Why does deuterium make the experiment possible? A carbon needs four different groups to be a stereocentre, and {{CH3CH2Cl}} has two identical hydrogens. Replacing one H with D makes the four groups different without changing the chemistry in any way that matters, so the flip becomes visible as a change from one enantiomer to the other.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>With a stereocentre, <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>. The product has the nucleophile on the face opposite the leaving group, so the nucleophile attacks from the <b>back</b>.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>With {{CH3Cl}} you cannot see the flip, because the three hydrogens look the same before and after. So the notes use a carbon that carries four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D (deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen). That carbon is a stereocentre.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start with the pure (<i>R</i>) chloride. The product is the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol. Not a mixture, and not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. The notes put it this way: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>.</p><p>That result tells you where the nucleophile came from. If hydroxide had attached on the same side that chloride left from, the three other groups would stay where they were and the product would have the same arrangement in space as the starting material. Instead the OH ends up on the opposite side from where Cl was, and the other three groups have swung through to make room. The nucleophile must attack the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group.</p><p>Inversion of the shape is not always a change of letter. Inversion describes the geometry: the new group sits on the opposite face. The letters (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>) come from priority rules. Here Cl is the top priority group before and OH is the top priority group after, and the other three keep their order (C, then D, then H), so the letter does flip from (<i>R</i>) to (<i>S</i>). If the nucleophile had a different priority rank from the leaving group, the letter could stay the same even though the carbon inverted. On an exam, draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite side (wedge becomes dash), then assign the letter fresh.</p><p>As a procedure: given one enantiomer of a chloride and a nucleophile, put the nucleophile on the face opposite the leaving group, let the other three groups swing through, then assign the letter afresh. The answer is one pure enantiomer, not a mixture and not a copy of the starting arrangement.</p><p>Question 2A of the past exam shows (<i>S</i>)-2-chloropentane reacting with sodium acetate in DMSO, and the product is drawn with the acetate on the opposite face. Question 3A asks which statements do not describe {{SN1}}, and the first choice is inversion, because inversion belongs to {{SN2}}. Stereochemistry matters in medicine for the same reason it matters here: the two enantiomers of a drug are different shapes, and an enzyme or receptor usually fits only one of them.</p><p>Deuterium makes the experiment possible. A carbon needs four different groups to be a stereocentre, and {{CH3CH2Cl}} has two identical hydrogens. Replacing one H with D makes the four groups different without changing the chemistry in any way that matters, so the flip becomes visible as a change from one enantiomer to the other.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>{{SN2}} substitution at a stereocentre is stereospecific: <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>. The nucleophile must approach from the <b>back</b>, along the axis opposite the leaving group.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>In {{CH3Cl}} the inversion is invisible, because the three hydrogens are equivalent before and after. The notes therefore use a stereocentre carrying four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D, where D is deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen. Isotopic substitution is enough to make the carbon stereogenic.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Pure (<i>R</i>) chloride gives the pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol: neither a racemic mixture nor the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. In the notes' words, <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>. The reaction is stereospecific, with complete inversion of configuration.</p><p>This constrains the geometry of approach. Attack on the same face that the leaving group occupies would leave the other three substituents where they were and produce the same spatial arrangement as the starting material. The observed product has the new group on the opposite face and the three remaining substituents swung through the plane they span, so the nucleophile must attack the carbon from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group, into the rear lobe of the σ* orbital of the C-Cl bond.</p><p>Inversion is a statement about geometry, not about the CIP descriptor. Here Cl outranks the other three substituents before, and OH outranks them after, with the other three keeping their order (C, then D, then H), so (<i>R</i>) becomes (<i>S</i>). If the nucleophile ranks differently from the leaving group, the descriptor can remain unchanged even though the carbon has inverted. The reliable procedure is to move the new bond to the opposite face (wedge becomes dash) and assign the descriptor afresh.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The inversion that the previous passage described cannot be observed with {{CH3Cl}}, because the three hydrogens are equivalent before and after the reaction. The notes therefore use a carbon carrying four different groups: Cl, {{CH3}}, H and D, where D is deuterium, the heavy isotope of hydrogen. Isotopic substitution is enough to make the carbon a stereocentre, and the isotope effect on the substitution itself is not significant for the stereochemical argument.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>Start from pure (<i>R</i>) chloride and the product is pure (<i>S</i>) alcohol: not a racemic mixture, and not the (<i>R</i>) alcohol. In the notes' words, <b>one enantiomer gives one enantiomer: inversion</b>. The reaction is stereospecific, and the stereochemical outcome is a fingerprint of the mechanism.</p><p>The result fixes the trajectory of the nucleophile. Attack on the face from which the leaving group departs would leave the other three substituents where they were and give a product with the same spatial arrangement as the starting material. The observed product has the new group on the opposite face, with the three remaining substituents swung through the plane they span, like an umbrella turned in the wind. The nucleophile must therefore attack from the <b>back</b>, directly opposite the leaving group, along the axis of the C-Cl bond, into the rear lobe of its σ* orbital. This stereochemical inversion is also known as the Walden inversion.</p><p>Geometric inversion and the CIP descriptor are different things. The letters (<i>R</i>) and (<i>S</i>) come from priority rules. Here Cl is the top priority before and OH is the top priority after, and the other three keep their order (C, then D, then H), so the descriptor flips from (<i>R</i>) to (<i>S</i>). If the nucleophile ranks differently from the leaving group, the descriptor can stay the same even though the carbon has inverted completely. The safe procedure is to draw the product by moving the new bond to the opposite face (wedge becomes dash) and then assign the descriptor from scratch.</p><p>The same logic is tested on the past exam. Question 2A shows (<i>S</i>)-2-chloropentane reacting with sodium acetate in DMSO, with the acetate drawn on the opposite face. Question 3A asks which statements do not describe {{SN1}}, and the first choice is inversion, because inversion belongs to {{SN2}}. Stereochemistry matters in medicine for the same reason: the two enantiomers of a drug differ in three-dimensional shape, and a chiral enzyme or receptor usually fits only one of them.</p><p>Finally, why does deuterium make the experiment possible? A carbon needs four different groups to be a stereocentre, and {{CH3CH2Cl}} has two identical hydrogens. Replacing one H with D makes the four groups different without changing the chemistry in any way that matters, so the flip becomes visible as a change from one enantiomer to the other.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 4 · The rate data: double either reactant and the rate doubles
  3: {
    "1.1": `<p>In this reaction, two pieces must meet for it to go. Double either piece and the reaction goes twice as fast. That is why it is called <b>{{SN2}}</b>: the 2 is for the two pieces.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>Chemists measured how fast one reaction goes. Hydrosulfide meets iodomethane. They tried different amounts of each piece.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>First they doubled the hydrosulfide. The reaction went twice as fast. Then they doubled the iodomethane instead. The reaction went twice as fast again. Then they doubled both. The reaction went four times as fast.</p><p>So the speed depends on both pieces. If you put in more of either one, the pieces bump into each other more often, and the reaction speeds up.</p><p>Think of a dance where every dancer needs a partner. If you add more dancers of either kind, more pairs form at the same time. The reaction needs both kinds, so more of either one means more happens each second.</p><p>This also tells us that the slowest step needs both pieces. A reaction like this is called <b>bimolecular</b>, which means two pieces take part. The name <b>{{SN2}}</b> stands for Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular. The 2 is for the two pieces that meet.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>How can you tell what happens inside a reaction? You cannot watch it. But you can measure how fast it goes. That speed is called the rate. And you can change how much of each piece you start with.</p><p>The lecture gives one reaction to test. A piece called hydrosulfide ({{HS-}}) meets iodomethane ({{H3C-I}}). The sulfur takes the place of the iodine on the carbon.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>Here are the speeds, in made-up units where the first one is 1.</p><ul><li>Trial A: 1 of each piece. The speed is 1.</li><li>Trial B: 2 of the hydrosulfide and 1 of the iodomethane. The speed is 2.</li><li>Trial C: 1 of the hydrosulfide and 2 of the iodomethane. The speed is 2.</li><li>Trial D: 2 of each. The speed is 4.</li></ul><p>Look at A and B. Only the hydrosulfide changed. It doubled, and the speed doubled. So the speed goes up in step with the hydrosulfide.</p><p>Look at A and C. Only the iodomethane changed. It doubled, and the speed doubled. So the speed goes up in step with the iodomethane too.</p><p>Trial D agrees. Both doubled, and the speed went up four times.</p><p>This is the trick for reading any table like this. Find two trials where only one amount changes. If doubling it doubles the speed, that piece counts once. If doubling it changes nothing, that piece is not in the slowest step. If doubling it makes the speed four times bigger, it counts twice. Then do the same for the other piece.</p><p>Because both pieces count once, we say the reaction is bimolecular. That is the word <b>bimolecular</b>: two pieces take part. The 2 in <b>{{SN2}}</b> stands for that. It does not mean two steps. It counts the pieces in the slowest step. An {{SN2}} reaction has one step with two pieces in it. Another kind, {{SN1}}, has more than one step, and only one piece in the slow one. That is a difference we will use later. In another table, for tert-butyl bromide and water, doubling the water changes nothing. That one difference separates {{SN1}} from {{SN2}}.</p><p>Why does speed depend on both pieces? A step can only go faster when you add more of something if that something takes part in it. Two pieces must bump into each other to react. Double either one, and there are twice as many bumps. So the slow step must be one piece of each kind meeting.</p><p>Now a use for this. Since the nucleophile is in the speed rule, a better nucleophile, or more of it, makes the reaction faster. One question on the past exam says that 1-bromobutane reacts faster with sodium methoxide in methanol than in methanol alone. The reason is that methoxide is a much better nucleophile than methanol. In an {{SN1}} reaction, the nucleophile does not change the speed at all.</p><p>Here is another use. Take two flasks of bromoethane with the same sodium azide. One has three times as much bromoethane. The one with more bromoethane goes faster, because the speed goes up with the amount of each piece.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>The rate depends on both reactants: double either one and the rate doubles. So the reaction is <b>bimolecular</b>, and the 2 in <b>{{SN2}}</b> stands for that.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>The professor gives the rate of one reaction at different starting amounts. Hydrosulfide ({{HS-}}) reacts with iodomethane ({{H3C-I}}). The numbers are relative.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>Trial A has 1 of each and a rate of 1. In trial B the {{HS-}} is doubled, and the rate is 2. In trial C the {{H3C-I}} is doubled, and the rate is 2. In trial D both are doubled, and the rate is 4.</p><p>Only {{HS-}} changed from A to B, and the rate doubled. So the rate goes up in step with [{{HS-}}]. Only {{CH3I}} changed from A to C, and the rate doubled, so the rate goes up in step with [{{CH3I}}] too. Trial D agrees: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>This gives the rate law: <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Each concentration is to the first power. The reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from: <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Both pieces are in the rate law. So the slowest step needs both the nucleophile and the electrophile.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>Chemists learn how a reaction works by measuring how fast it goes. The professor gives the rate of one reaction at different starting amounts. Hydrosulfide ({{HS-}}) reacts with iodomethane ({{H3C-I}}).</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>All numbers are relative.</p><ul><li>Trial A: 1 of {{HS-}}, 1 of {{H3C-I}}, rate 1.</li><li>Trial B: 2 of {{HS-}}, 1 of {{H3C-I}}, rate 2.</li><li>Trial C: 1 of {{HS-}}, 2 of {{H3C-I}}, rate 2.</li><li>Trial D: 2 of each, rate 4.</li></ul><p>Compare A with B. Only {{HS-}} doubled, and the rate doubled. So the rate goes up in step with [{{HS-}}]. Compare A with C. Only {{CH3I}} doubled, and the rate doubled. So the rate goes up in step with [{{CH3I}}] too. Trial D agrees: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>The rate law is <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Two concentrations appear, each to the first power. The reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from: <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>To read any rate table, find two trials in which only one concentration changes. If doubling the concentration doubles the rate, the order in that species is 1. If the rate does not change, the order is 0, and that species is not in the slow step. If the rate goes up four times, the order is 2. Then repeat for the other species. For tert-butyl bromide and water, doubling the water changes nothing. That single difference separates {{SN1}} from {{SN2}}.</p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Since both species are in the rate law, the slowest step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile, and so does the transition state. The 2 in {{SN2}} is not the number of steps. It counts the species that take part in the rate-determining step. An {{SN2}} reaction has one step with two species in it. An {{SN1}} reaction has more than one step, with one species in the slow step.</p><p>Why does the rate law show that the nucleophile and electrophile meet in the slow step? A step goes faster when you add more of something only if that something takes part in the step. Doubling either reactant doubles the number of collisions between them, and the rate follows. So the slow step must be a collision of one nucleophile with one electrophile.</p><p>Here is the same idea in a short rule. Look at which concentrations are in the rate law. Those species take part in the slow step. A species that is not in the rate law, like water in the tert-butyl bromide case, does not take part in it. This is why the rate law is so useful for working out how a reaction happens.</p><p>The nucleophile is in the rate law, so more nucleophile, or a better one, makes an {{SN2}} reaction faster. One question on the past exam says that 1-bromobutane with sodium methoxide in methanol is faster than 1-bromobutane in methanol alone. The reason is that {{CH3O-}} is a far better nucleophile than neutral {{CH3OH}}. The rate of {{SN1}} does not depend on the nucleophile at all. Likewise, with the same sodium azide, a flask with three times as much bromoethane is faster, because the rate goes up with the concentration of the electrophile.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>The rate depends on the nucleophile and the electrophile, each to the first power: <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. The reaction is <b>bimolecular</b>, so it is called <b>{{SN2}}</b>.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group: {{HS-}} reacts with {{H3C-I}}, and sulfur ends up on the carbon in place of iodine.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The professor gives the rate at different starting concentrations (all numbers are relative):</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>Compare A with B: only {{HS-}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is proportional to [{{HS-}}]. Compare A with C: only {{CH3I}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is also proportional to [{{CH3I}}]. Trial D confirms it: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>This gives the rate law <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Each concentration is to the first power, so the reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from: <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Both species are in the rate law, so the slowest step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>How do chemists find out which species take part in the slow step of a reaction? They measure the rate at different starting concentrations. A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group: {{HS-}} reacts with {{H3C-I}}, and sulfur takes the place of iodine on carbon.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The professor gives the rates (all numbers are relative):</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>Compare A with B: only {{HS-}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is proportional to [{{HS-}}]. Compare A with C: only {{CH3I}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is also proportional to [{{CH3I}}]. Trial D confirms it: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>The result is the rate law <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Two concentrations appear, each to the first power. The reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from: <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>You can read any rate table the same way. Find two trials in which only one concentration changes, and divide the rates. If doubling the concentration doubles the rate, the order in that species is 1. If the rate does not change, the order is 0, and that species is not in the rate-determining step. If the rate goes up four times, the order is 2. Then repeat for the other species. Compare this table with the one for tert-butyl bromide and water on 11/10: there, doubling water changes nothing, and that single difference separates {{SN1}} from {{SN2}}.</p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Since both species are in the rate law, the rate-determining step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile, and so does the transition state. The 2 in {{SN2}} is not the number of steps. It counts the species that take part in the rate-determining step. An {{SN2}} reaction has one step with two species in it, while an {{SN1}} reaction has more than one step, with one species in the slow step.</p><p>Why does a rate law with both concentrations in it tell you that the two species meet in the slow step? A step can only go faster when you add more of something if that something takes part in the step. Doubling either reactant doubles the number of collisions between them, and the rate follows. So the slow step must be a collision of one nucleophile with one electrophile.</p><p>The rate law is also a tool for making reactions faster. Because the nucleophile is in the rate law, a higher concentration of nucleophile, or a better nucleophile, speeds up an {{SN2}} reaction. Question 2A of the past exam uses this: 1-bromobutane with sodium methoxide in methanol is faster than in methanol alone, because {{CH3O-}} is a far better nucleophile than neutral {{CH3OH}}. The same logic applies to concentration. With the same sodium azide, bromoethane at 0.30 M reacts faster than at 0.10 M, because the rate is proportional to the electrophile concentration. Question 3A turns it around: the rate of {{SN1}} does not depend on the nucleophile at all.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>Doubling either the nucleophile or the electrophile doubles the rate, so <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. The reaction is <b>second order</b> and <b>bimolecular</b>, hence <b>{{SN2}}</b>.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group: {{HS-}} reacts with {{H3C-I}}, and sulfur takes the place of iodine.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The professor gives the rate measured at different starting concentrations (all numbers are relative):</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>From A to B only {{HS-}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is proportional to [{{HS-}}]. From A to C only {{CH3I}} doubled and the rate doubled, so it is proportional to [{{CH3I}}] as well. Trial D confirms it: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>The rate law is <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Two concentrations appear, each to the first power: the reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That gives the name <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Because both species are in it, that step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile, and so does its transition state.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>The rate law gives evidence about the mechanism of a reaction, and it comes from measuring the rate at different starting concentrations. A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group: {{HS-}} reacts with {{H3C-I}}, and sulfur takes the place of iodine on carbon.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The professor gives these relative rates:</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>From A to B only {{HS-}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is proportional to [{{HS-}}]. From A to C only {{CH3I}} doubled and the rate doubled, so the rate is proportional to [{{CH3I}}] as well. Trial D confirms it: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>The rate law is <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Two concentrations appear, each to the first power: the reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from: <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>To read any rate table, find two trials in which only one concentration changes, and divide the rates. If doubling the concentration doubles the rate, the order in that species is 1. If the rate does not change, the order is 0, and the species is not in the rate-determining step. If the rate rises four times, the order is 2. Then repeat for the other species. In the table for tert-butyl bromide and water on 11/10, doubling water changes nothing, and that one difference separates {{SN1}} from {{SN2}}.</p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Since both species appear in the rate law, that step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile, and so does the transition state. The 2 in {{SN2}} counts the species in the rate-determining step, not the number of steps: {{SN2}} has one step with two species in it, and {{SN1}} has more than one step with one species in the slow step.</p><p>Why does having both concentrations in the rate law show that the nucleophile and electrophile meet in the slow step? A step speeds up when you add more of a species only if that species takes part in it. Doubling either reactant doubles the number of collisions between them, and the rate follows. So the slow step is a collision of one nucleophile with one electrophile.</p><p>In short, the rate law tells you which species take part in the slow step. A species that does not appear in it, like water in the tert-butyl bromide case, is not in that step. That is why measuring rates is a standard way to find out how a reaction happens.</p><p>This makes the rate law a tool for speeding reactions up. A higher concentration of nucleophile, or a better nucleophile, makes an {{SN2}} reaction faster. In Question 2A of the past exam, 1-bromobutane with sodium methoxide in methanol is faster than in methanol alone, because {{CH3O-}} is a far better nucleophile than neutral {{CH3OH}}. Concentration works the same way: with sodium azide fixed, bromoethane at 0.30 M reacts faster than at 0.10 M. Question 3A turns it around: the rate of {{SN1}} does not depend on the nucleophile at all.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>The rate law <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b> is first order in each reactant, so the reaction is <b>second order</b>, or <b>bimolecular</b>, and the <b>rate-determining step</b> involves both species: <b>{{SN2}}</b>.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group: {{HS-}} reacts with {{H3C-I}} to give a carbon-sulfur bond and {{I-}}.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The professor gives the rate measured at different starting concentrations (all numbers are relative):</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>Compare A with B: only {{HS-}} doubled, and the rate doubled. So the rate is proportional to [{{HS-}}]. Compare A with C: only {{CH3I}} doubled, and the rate doubled. So the rate is also proportional to [{{CH3I}}]. Trial D confirms it: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p><b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b></p><p>Two concentrations appear in the rate law, each to the first power: the reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from. <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Since both species are in the rate law, the rate-determining step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile, and so does the transition state.</p><p>To read any rate table, find two trials in which only one concentration changes. Divide the rates. If doubling the concentration doubles the rate, the order in that species is 1. If the rate does not change, the order is 0, and that species is not in the rate-determining step. If the rate goes up four times, the order is 2. Then repeat for the other species. Compare this table with the one for tert-butyl bromide and water on 11/10: there, doubling water changes nothing, and that single difference is what separates {{SN1}} from {{SN2}}.</p><p>The 2 in {{SN2}} is not the number of steps. It counts the species that take part in the rate-determining step. An {{SN2}} reaction has one step with two species in it. An {{SN1}} reaction has more than one step, with one species in the slow step. Read the number as the molecularity of the slow step and you will not confuse the two.</p><p>A step can only go faster when you add more of something if that something takes part in the step. Doubling either reactant doubles the number of collisions between them, and the rate follows, so the slow step is a collision of one nucleophile with one electrophile.</p><p>In short, the rate law tells you which species take part in the slow step. A species that does not appear in it, like water in the tert-butyl bromide case, is not in that step. That is why measuring rates is a standard way to find out how a reaction happens.</p><p>Because the nucleophile is in the rate law, a higher concentration of nucleophile, or a better nucleophile, speeds up an {{SN2}} reaction. Question 2A of the past exam uses exactly this: 1-bromobutane with sodium methoxide in methanol is faster than 1-bromobutane in methanol alone, because {{CH3O-}} is a far better nucleophile than neutral {{CH3OH}}. In the same way, with the same sodium azide, bromoethane at 0.30 M reacts faster than at 0.10 M. Question 3A turns it around: the rate of {{SN1}} does not depend on the nucleophile at all.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>The experimental rate law <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b> is <b>second order</b> overall and first order in each reactant. It is consistent with a <b>bimolecular</b> <b>rate-determining step</b>, which is what the <b>{{SN2}}</b> label denotes.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group:</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The rate of {{HS-}} with {{H3C-I}} was measured at different starting concentrations (relative numbers), in effect by the method of initial rates:</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>A to B varies only [{{HS-}}], and the rate doubles: first order in {{HS-}}. A to C varies only [{{CH3I}}], and the rate doubles: first order in {{CH3I}}. Trial D, with both doubled and the rate times four, is consistent with the product form.</p><p>The empirical rate law is <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Each concentration enters to the first power, so the reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. The name follows: <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>A rate law reflects the species involved up to and including the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Here both nucleophile and electrophile appear, so that step, and its transition state, contain both. The 2 in {{SN2}} is the molecularity of the rate-determining step, not the number of steps: {{SN2}} has a single step with two species, whereas {{SN1}} has more than one step with one species in the slow step. Kinetics cannot prove a mechanism, but a second-order law of this form is what a bimolecular rate-determining step predicts, because doubling either reactant doubles the collision frequency.</p><p>The same law explains why a better or more concentrated nucleophile accelerates {{SN2}}. The rate of {{SN1}}, by contrast, is independent of the nucleophile.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>Kinetics is an experimental route from a reaction to its mechanism. A second example, with a different nucleophile and leaving group: {{HS-}} reacts with {{H3C-I}} to give a carbon-sulfur bond and {{I-}}.</p>
[[rxn:[SH-] + CI>>CS + [I-]]]
<p>The professor gives rates measured at different starting concentrations, in effect by the method of initial rates (all numbers are relative):</p><table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[{{HS-}}]</th><th>[{{H3C-I}}]</th><th>rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr></table><p>Compare A with B: only {{HS-}} is varied, and the rate doubles. So the order in {{HS-}} is 1, and the rate is proportional to [{{HS-}}]. Compare A with C: only {{CH3I}} is varied, and the rate doubles, so the order in {{CH3I}} is also 1. Trial D confirms the product form: both doubled, rate times four.</p><p>The empirical rate law is <b>rate = <i>k</i>[{{HS-}}][{{H3C-I}}]</b>. Two concentrations appear, each to the first power: the reaction is <b>second order</b> overall, or <b>bimolecular</b>. That is where the name comes from. <b>{{SN2}}: Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular (2).</b></p><p>The general method is to find two trials in which only one concentration changes and divide the rates. If doubling the concentration doubles the rate, the order in that species is 1. If the rate does not change, the order is 0, and the species is not involved in or before the rate-determining step. If the rate rises four times, the order is 2. Then repeat for the other species. Compare the tert-butyl bromide and water table of 11/10: there, doubling water changes nothing, and that single difference separates {{SN1}} from {{SN2}}.</p><p>The rate law describes the slowest step, the <b>rate-determining step</b>. Since both species are in the rate law, that step involves both the nucleophile and the electrophile, and so does the transition state. The 2 in {{SN2}} is not the number of steps. It is the molecularity of the rate-determining step, the number of species taking part. An {{SN2}} reaction has a single step with two species in it. An {{SN1}} reaction has more than one step, with one species in the slow step. For an elementary step, molecularity and kinetic order coincide, which is why the two words are interchangeable here. For a multistep reaction they need not be.</p><p>Why does a rate law containing both concentrations show that the nucleophile and electrophile meet in the slow step? A step can accelerate with added concentration of a species only if that species takes part in it. Doubling either reactant doubles the frequency of collisions between them, and the rate follows. So the slow step is a collision of one nucleophile with one electrophile. Strictly, kinetics is consistent with a mechanism and cannot prove it, which is why the stereochemical evidence from the previous passage matters as an independent check.</p><p>The rate law is also a practical tool. Because the nucleophile is in it, a higher concentration of nucleophile, or a better nucleophile, speeds up an {{SN2}} reaction. Question 2A of the past exam uses exactly this: 1-bromobutane with sodium methoxide in methanol is faster than in methanol alone, because {{CH3O-}} is a far better nucleophile than neutral {{CH3OH}}. Concentration works in the same way: with the same sodium azide, bromoethane at 0.30 M reacts faster than at 0.10 M, because the rate is first order in the electrophile. Question 3A turns it around: the rate of {{SN1}} does not depend on the nucleophile at all.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 5 · Fact 1 of the mechanism: concerted, one step, no intermediate
  4: {
    "1.1": `<p>In this reaction, everything happens at the same time, in a single step. That is called <b>concerted</b>. There is no stopping place in the middle.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>Some changes happen in one go. A ball rolls up one hill and down the other side. It does not stop on the way.</p><p>The reaction in this chapter, {{SN2}}, is like that ball. It is a <b>concerted</b> reaction. That means everything happens at the same time, in one step.</p><p>One new bond starts to form. At the very same time, one old bond starts to break. One group comes in as another group goes out.</p><p>There is no stopping place in the middle. Nothing waits there, half finished. The energy picture has one hill and no valley.</p><p>At the top of the hill, the new bond is half made and the old bond is half broken. You cannot pick that moment up or keep it in a jar. The height of the hill decides how fast the reaction goes.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>Some changes happen in one go. Picture a ball rolling over a single hill. It goes up, it reaches the top, and it rolls down the other side. It never stops in the middle. It is one smooth trip.</p><p>The reaction in this chapter is called {{SN2}}. It works like that ball. It is a <b>concerted</b> reaction. That means everything happens at the same time, in a single step.</p><p>Here is what goes on. One molecule has a carbon atom with a group on it that is ready to leave. Another molecule, the nucleophile, is the group that comes in and makes a new bond to that carbon. The new bond starts to form. At the very same time, the old bond to the leaving group starts to break. It is like one child sliding onto a bench just as another child slides off.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>Two things never happen. The leaving group never goes away first and leaves the carbon waiting alone. If that happened, the carbon would have a plus charge and be called a carbocation. And the carbon never holds five full bonds at once. It holds only half bonds to the two groups that are swapping.</p><p>Chemists draw the energy of the reaction as a picture. It starts low on the left, climbs, and ends on the right.</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>This picture has one hill and no valley. The numbers on it are only a sketch. What matters is the shape.</p><p>The top of the hill is a special moment. The new bond is half made and the old bond is half broken. Chemists call it the transition state. It is not something you can pick up or keep in a jar.</p><p>A valley between two hills would be a place where something real could rest for a while. That is called an intermediate. This reaction has no valley, so it has no intermediate.</p><p>The height of the hill is the energy the reaction needs to get over the top. It is called the activation energy, {{Ea}}. It decides how fast the reaction goes. To measure it going backward, start from the products instead and go up to the same top.</p><p>How do we know it is one step? If the leaving group went first, only one molecule would matter for the speed. But tests show the speed depends on both molecules. A carbocation would also be flat, so the new group could come from either side and make both mirror images. Tests show only one mirror image, the flipped one. So the carbocation story does not fit, and the one-step story does.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>In an {{SN2}} reaction, everything happens at once in a single step. The reaction is <b>concerted</b>, with no <b>intermediate</b>, no resting stage in the middle.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>An {{SN2}} reaction is <b>concerted</b>: it happens all at once, in one step, with no <b>intermediate</b>. An intermediate would be a real molecule that sits in the middle for a while.</p><p>The nucleophile starts to bond to the carbon at the same moment the leaving group starts to leave. The leaving group never leaves first and lets a carbocation form. The carbon also never has five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>The energy picture has <b>one hump</b>:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights are only a sketch. The shape is what matters. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>. At that moment the new bond is half made and the old bond is half broken. It is only a moment on the way, not a molecule you can keep. An intermediate would show up as a valley between two humps, and an {{SN2}} picture has no valley. The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, and it sets how fast the reaction goes. For the reverse reaction, the height is measured from the products up to the same top.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>An {{SN2}} reaction is <b>concerted</b>. That means it happens all at once, in a single step, and no <b>intermediate</b> forms. An intermediate is a real molecule that exists for a while in the middle of a reaction, between two steps. {{SN2}} has no middle stage like that.</p><p>Here is what happens. The nucleophile is the group that brings electrons and bonds to carbon. The leaving group is the group that goes away. The nucleophile begins to bond to the carbon at the same time that the leaving group begins to leave. They trade places in one smooth motion.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>Two things never happen. The leaving group never leaves first. That would leave a carbocation, a carbon with a plus charge, waiting for the nucleophile. And the carbon never holds five full bonds at the same time.</p><p>Chemists show this with an energy diagram. The reaction starts low, climbs to a peak, and comes down to the products. That is <b>one hump</b>.</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights in this sketch are only a guide. The shape is what counts. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>. It is the highest energy arrangement along the path. In it the new bond is half made and the old bond is half broken. It is not a compound you can isolate. It lasts about as long as one bond vibration, and chemists draw it in square brackets with a double dagger and dotted bonds.</p><p>An intermediate looks different on a diagram. It is a valley between two humps, a real species with full bonds that lives long enough to react further, for example a carbocation. One hump means one transition state and no intermediate. Two humps mean two transition states with one intermediate between them. The {{SN2}} diagram has no valley.</p><p>The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}. It sets the rate: a taller hump means a slower reaction. For the reverse reaction, products going back to starting materials, the activation energy is measured from the products up to the same peak.</p><p>Experiments agree with a single step. If the leaving group left first, the slow step would involve only the molecule that carries it, and the nucleophile would not appear in the rate law. But the measured rate law has both. A flat carbocation could also be attacked from either face, which would give both mirror-image products. The experiment gives only the inverted one.</p><p>A past exam question shows four energy diagrams and asks which one is an exothermic {{SN1}}. Two of the four have a single hump, so they are concerted reactions, {{SN2}} or {{E2}}. The answer is the two-hump diagram that ends lower than it starts.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>An {{SN2}} reaction happens in a single step: the nucleophile bonds to carbon while the leaving group leaves. It is <b>concerted</b>, so there is no <b>intermediate</b>.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>The simplest way to explain the rate law is a mechanism with one step in which everything happens together. Chemists call this a <b>concerted</b> reaction: all at once, with no intermediate. An <b>intermediate</b> is a real molecule that forms in the middle of a reaction and lasts long enough to react further.</p><p>In {{SN2}}, the nucleophile starts to bond to carbon while the leaving group starts to leave. The leaving group never leaves first, which would leave a carbocation waiting. Carbon also never has five full bonds at once.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>The energy diagram has <b>one hump</b> between the starting materials and the products:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights are only a sketch, and the shape is what matters. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>, the highest energy arrangement along the path. There the new bond is half made and the old bond is half broken. It cannot be isolated as a compound. An intermediate would be a valley between two humps, and an {{SN2}} diagram has no valley. The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, and it sets the rate. For the reverse reaction, measure from the products up to the same top instead.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>The rate law for {{SN2}} contains both the nucleophile and the molecule that carries the leaving group. The simplest mechanism that fits is one step in which everything happens together. Chemists call this a <b>concerted</b> reaction: all at once, with no intermediate. An <b>intermediate</b> is a real molecule that forms in the middle of a reaction, after one step and before the next, and lasts long enough to react further.</p><p>In {{SN2}}, the nucleophile begins to bond to carbon while the leaving group begins to leave. There is never a moment when the leaving group has gone and the carbon is left waiting. That waiting carbon, with a plus charge, would be a carbocation. There is also never a moment when carbon holds five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>Chemists describe the energy changes with a diagram. The energy of the molecules is plotted against the progress of the reaction. For {{SN2}} the diagram has <b>one hump</b> between the starting materials and the products:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights in this sketch are only schematic. What matters is the shape. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>. It is the highest energy arrangement along the path, where the new bond is half made and the old bond is half broken. It lasts about as long as one bond vibration, so it is not a compound you can isolate. Chemists draw it in square brackets with a double dagger and dotted lines for the partial bonds.</p><p>An intermediate is the opposite kind of thing. It sits in an energy valley, with full bonds, and lives long enough to be attacked from either side or to rearrange. A carbocation is an example. One hump means one transition state and no intermediate. Two humps mean two transition states with one intermediate between them. An {{SN2}} diagram has no valley.</p><p>The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}. It sets the rate, because a higher hump means fewer collisions have enough energy to get over it. For the reverse reaction, going from products back to starting materials, you measure from the products up to the same top: the energy of the transition state minus the energy of the products.</p><p>Why not a carbocation? If the leaving group left first, the slow step would involve only the molecule that carries it, and the nucleophile would not appear in the rate law. The measured rate law has both. A flat carbocation would also be attacked from both faces and give both mirror-image products, but the experiment gives only the inverted one.</p><p>A past exam question shows four energy diagrams and asks which is an exothermic {{SN1}}. Two of the four have a single hump, so they are concerted: {{SN2}} or {{E2}}, one exothermic and one endothermic. The answer is the two-hump diagram that ends lower than it starts.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>In {{SN2}} the nucleophile attacks and the leaving group leaves in a single step. The reaction is <b>concerted</b>: it has one <b>transition state</b> and no <b>intermediate</b>.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>The simplest mechanism that fits the rate law is a single step in which everything happens together. This is a <b>concerted</b> reaction: all at once, with no <b>intermediate</b>. In {{SN2}} the nucleophile begins to bond to carbon while the leaving group begins to leave. The leaving group never departs first, which would leave a carbocation, and carbon never holds five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>The energy diagram has <b>one hump</b> from the starting materials to the products:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights are only schematic, so look at the shape. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>, the highest energy arrangement along the path, with the new bond half made and the old bond half broken. You cannot isolate it as a compound. An intermediate would be a valley between two humps, and an {{SN2}} diagram has no valley. The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, which sets the rate. For the reverse reaction the barrier is measured from the products up to the top.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>The simplest mechanism that fits the {{SN2}} rate law is one step in which everything happens together. This is a <b>concerted</b> reaction: all at once, with no intermediate. In {{SN2}} the nucleophile begins to bond to carbon while the leaving group begins to leave. There is never a moment when the leaving group has gone and carbon is waiting: that species would be a carbocation. There is also never a moment when carbon holds five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>On an energy diagram the reaction has <b>one hump</b> from start to product:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights in this sketch are only schematic, and the shape is what matters. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>: the highest energy arrangement along the path, where the new bond is half made and the old one is half broken. It is an energy maximum, not a compound you can isolate. It lasts about as long as one bond vibration, and it is drawn in square brackets with a double dagger and partial (dotted) bonds.</p><p>An <b>intermediate</b> is the opposite: an energy minimum, a valley between two humps. It is a real species with full bonds, such as a carbocation, that lives long enough to be attacked from either side or to rearrange. One hump means one transition state and no intermediate. Two humps mean two transition states with one intermediate between them. An {{SN2}} diagram has no valley.</p><p>The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, and it sets the rate. The reverse reaction has its own barrier, measured from the products: the energy of the transition state minus the energy of the products. The forward and reverse barriers differ by the energy difference between starting materials and products.</p><p>The rate law already excludes a carbocation. If the leaving group left first, the slow step would involve only the electrophile, and the nucleophile would not appear in the rate law, but the measured rate law has both. A flat carbocation would also be attacked from both faces and give both enantiomers, while the experiment gives only the inverted one.</p><p>This is tested directly on the past exam. Question 3B shows four energy diagrams and asks which is an exothermic {{SN1}}. Two of the four have a single hump: those are concerted, {{SN2}} or {{E2}}, one exothermic and one endothermic. The answer is the two-hump diagram that ends lower than it starts. If you can sketch the {{SN2}} diagram from memory and say why it has no valley, that question takes seconds.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is a <b>concerted</b> reaction: one step, one <b>transition state</b> and no <b>intermediate</b>, so its energy diagram has <b>one hump</b>.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>The simplest mechanism that fits the rate law is one step in which everything happens together: <b>concerted</b>, all at once, no intermediate. The nucleophile begins to bond to carbon while the leaving group begins to leave. There is never a moment when the leaving group has gone and carbon is waiting (that would be a carbocation), and never a moment when carbon holds five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>The energy diagram has <b>one hump</b> from start to product:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights in this sketch are only schematic. What matters is the shape. The top of the hump is the <b>transition state</b>: the highest energy arrangement along the path, where the new bond is half made and the old one is half broken. It is an energy maximum, it lasts about as long as one bond vibration, and it is not a compound you can isolate. It is drawn in square brackets with a double dagger and partial (dotted) bonds.</p><p>An <b>intermediate</b> would be a valley between two humps, an energy minimum. It is a real species with full bonds, such as a carbocation, that lives long enough to be attacked from either side or to rearrange. One hump means one transition state and no intermediate. Two humps mean two transition states with one intermediate between them. An {{SN2}} diagram has no valley.</p><p>The height of the hump above the starting materials is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, and it sets the rate. For the reverse reaction, read the barrier from the products: the energy of the transition state minus the energy of the products. It is the same peak, reached from the other side, so the forward and reverse barriers differ by the energy difference between starting materials and products.</p><p>The rate law already rules out a carbocation. If the leaving group left first, the slow step would involve only the electrophile, and the nucleophile would not appear in the rate law. The measured rate law has both. A flat carbocation would also be attacked from both faces and give both enantiomers, but the experiment gives only the inverted one.</p><p>Question 3B of the past exam rests on this. It shows four energy diagrams and asks which is an exothermic {{SN1}}. Two of the four have a single hump: those are concerted reactions, {{SN2}} or {{E2}}, one exothermic and one endothermic. The answer is the two-hump diagram that ends lower than it starts. If you can sketch the {{SN2}} diagram from memory and say why it has no valley, that question takes seconds.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is a single elementary step: nucleophilic attack and loss of the leaving group are <b>concerted</b>, passing through one <b>transition state</b> and no <b>intermediate</b>.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>The {{SN2}} rate law is consistent with the simplest mechanism, a single elementary step in which attack by the nucleophile and loss of the leaving group occur together: a <b>concerted</b> process with no <b>intermediate</b>. Bond formation and bond cleavage overlap in time, though they need not be equally advanced at the transition state. Carbon is never a free carbocation and never carries five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>The reaction coordinate diagram shows <b>one hump</b>:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights are schematic. The summit is the <b>transition state</b>, the maximum along the reaction coordinate, with the new bond partly formed and the old one partly broken. It is not a species that can be isolated. An intermediate is a local minimum, a valley between two humps, and {{SN2}} has none. The height of the hump above the reactants is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, which sets the rate. For the reverse reaction the barrier is the energy of the transition state minus the energy of the products.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The {{SN2}} rate law, first order in each reactant, is consistent with the simplest mechanism: a single elementary step in which attack by the nucleophile and loss of the leaving group occur together. This is a <b>concerted</b> process with no <b>intermediate</b>. Bond formation and bond cleavage overlap in time, although they need not be equally advanced at the transition state. Carbon is never a free carbocation, and it never carries five full bonds.</p>
[[mech:sn2-iodomethane-hydrosulfide]]
<p>On a reaction coordinate diagram the process has <b>one hump</b> from reactants to products:</p>
[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10|TS=transition state]]
<p>The heights are schematic, and the shape carries the information. The summit is the <b>transition state</b>. On the potential energy surface it is a first-order saddle point: a maximum along the reaction coordinate and a minimum in every other direction. The new bond is partly formed and the old one partly broken. The transition state is not a species that can be isolated, since it persists for about one bond vibration. It is written in square brackets with a double dagger and partial bonds. Both the bond to the nucleophile and the bond to the leaving group are fractional in the transition state.</p><p>An <b>intermediate</b>, by contrast, is a local minimum: a valley between two humps, a real species with full bonds, such as a carbocation, that lives long enough to be attacked from either face or to rearrange. One hump means one transition state and no intermediate; two humps mean two transition states with one intermediate between them. The {{SN2}} diagram has no valley.</p><p>The height of the hump above the reactants is the activation energy, {{Ea}}, and it sets the rate. In transition state theory the rate constant depends exponentially on the free energy of activation, so a small change in barrier height changes the rate a great deal. The reverse reaction passes through the same transition state, by microscopic reversibility, so its barrier is the energy of the transition state minus the energy of the products.</p><p>The kinetics and the stereochemistry both argue against a stepwise path through a carbocation. If ionization came first and were slow, the slow step would involve only the electrophile, and the rate law would be independent of the nucleophile. The measured rate law contains both. A planar carbocation would also be captured from either face and give both enantiomers, while the experiment gives only the inverted one.</p><p>The same distinction is tested on the past exam. Question 3B shows four energy diagrams and asks which is an exothermic {{SN1}}. Two of the four have a single hump and so describe concerted reactions, {{SN2}} or {{E2}}, one exothermic and one endothermic. The answer is the two-hump diagram that ends lower than it starts.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 6 · Fact 2: inversion at the electrophilic atom, and the shape of the transition state
  5: {
    "1.1": `<p>The three other groups on the carbon flip over, like an umbrella turned inside out by the wind. The new group comes in from the back, directly opposite the group that leaves. This flip is called <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>A carbon atom can hold four groups around it. Think of three of them as the ribs of an umbrella.</p><p>In this reaction, one group leaves and a new group comes in. The new group comes in from the back. It is directly opposite the group that leaves.</p><p>The three other groups stay on the carbon. At the start they lean away from the group that leaves. In the middle they lie flat. At the end they lean the other way. They have turned inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind. This flip is called <b>inversion</b>.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>In the flat moment, the new group and the old group are both joined to the carbon by half bonds. A half bond is a bond that is only partly made or partly broken. So the carbon has three ordinary bonds and two half bonds for an instant. That moment is very short. You cannot keep it in a jar.</p><p>If the new group has a negative charge, the charge is shared in that moment. Some is on the new group and some is on the group that leaves.</p><p>Only the carbon that held the leaving group flips. Every other part of the molecule stays as it was.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>Imagine an umbrella. A strong wind comes and turns it inside out. The ribs that pointed one way now point the other way. In one kind of reaction, the same thing happens to a carbon atom.</p><p>A carbon atom can hold four groups. In this reaction, one group leaves and a new group takes its place. The new group does not come in from the side or from the front. It comes in from the back, exactly opposite the group that is leaving. The two groups and the carbon are on a straight line, 180° apart.</p><p>The other three groups stay on the carbon. Watch what they do. At the start they lean away from the group that is leaving. In the middle they lie flat, in one plane through the carbon, with 120° between them. At the end they lean away from the new group, toward the side where the old group used to be. They have turned inside out, just like the umbrella. This flip is called <b>inversion</b>.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>In the flat moment, the new group and the old group are both joined to the carbon by half bonds. A half bond is a bond that is only partly made or partly broken. Chemists draw half bonds as dots and write the moment like this: [Nu···C···LG] ‡. Nu is the group coming in, which is called the nucleophile. LG is the leaving group. For an instant the carbon has three ordinary bonds and two half bonds. That is only a passing moment. You cannot catch it and keep it.</p><p>If the new group has a negative charge, the charge is shared in that moment. Some of it is on the new group and some is on the leaving group. Take methyl iodide, {{CH3I}}, meeting hydroxide, {{HO-}}. In the flat moment, the oxygen and the iodine are on opposite sides of the carbon. Each is joined to the carbon by a dotted bond. Each carries a little negative charge, written δ-. The three hydrogens lie in one flat plane.</p><p>Why does the new group come in from the back? It brings a pair of electrons. They need an empty place to go on the carbon. The biggest empty place is on the back side, away from the leaving group. So that is where the electrons fit best. When they go in, they also loosen the bond to the leaving group, so it lets go just as the new bond forms. The front is a bad place. The leaving group is already there, and it is full of electrons. Two groups full of electrons on the same side would push each other away.</p><p>Five groups around one carbon is a crowd. If the three groups that stay are hydrogens, there is room. If they are bigger groups made of carbon, the new group cannot get to the back of the carbon, and the reaction does not go well. That is why a carbon with more carbon groups on it reacts more slowly, and a carbon with three carbon groups does not react this way at all.</p><p>Only the carbon that held the leaving group flips. If the molecule has other carbons with their own arrangement of groups, those stay exactly as they were.</p><p>So the two groups must be 180° apart. The electrons go into the big empty place at the back, and that also keeps the two electron-rich groups as far apart as they can be.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>In {{SN2}}, the nucleophile attacks the carbon from the side opposite the leaving group. The three other groups flip over like an umbrella in the wind. This is <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>The second feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom. The middle moment is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>, where Nu is the nucleophile and LG is the leaving group.</p><p>The nucleophile is on one side of the carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. Both are held by half bonds, shown as dots. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie flat in one plane through the carbon. For an instant the carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>: three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups through the reaction. At the start they lean away from the leaving group. In the middle they are flat. At the end they lean away from the nucleophile. They have turned inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind. That is inversion.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>If the nucleophile has a negative charge, the charge is shared in the middle moment: part on the nucleophile and part on the leaving group, shown as δ-. Only the carbon that held the leaving group is inverted. Any other stereocentre in the molecule is not changed.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>The second feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom. The carbon that the nucleophile attacks flips its three other groups, like an umbrella turned inside out by the wind.</p><p>The middle moment of the reaction is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>. Nu is the nucleophile, the group coming in. LG is the leaving group. The dots are half bonds, bonds that are partly made or partly broken. The nucleophile is on one side of the carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie flat in one plane through the carbon. In a drawing, {{R1}} goes straight up, {{R2}} on a dash and {{R3}} on a wedge. For an instant the carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>: three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups through the reaction. At the start they lean away from the leaving group, toward the side the nucleophile will come from. In the middle they are flat. At the end they lean away from the nucleophile, toward the side the leaving group left. They have turned inside out. That is inversion.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>If the nucleophile has a negative charge, the charge is shared in the middle moment. Part of it is on the nucleophile and part is on the leaving group. Take the reaction of {{CH3I}} with {{HO-}}. In the middle moment, O and I are on opposite sides of the carbon, each joined to it by a dotted bond, with a partial negative charge, δ-, on both. The three hydrogens lie in one plane.</p><p>Why does the nucleophile come in from the back? It gives a lone pair of electrons to an empty orbital on the carbon. The lowest empty orbital of the bond between the carbon and the leaving group is called its antibonding orbital, and its biggest lobe points out from the back of the carbon, away from the leaving group. So the overlap is best from the back. Putting electrons into this orbital also weakens the bond to the leaving group, which is why that bond breaks as the new one forms. An attack from the front would put the nucleophile in a node, next to the electron-rich leaving group.</p><p>The shape changes along the way. At the start the carbon is {{sp3}} and tetrahedral, with angles near 109.5°. In the middle moment the three groups that stay are in a plane with 120° between them, and the nucleophile and the leaving group sit above and below that plane on a straight line. At the end the carbon is {{sp3}} and tetrahedral again, pointing the other way.</p><p>Only the carbon that carried the leaving group is inverted. If the molecule has another stereocentre, it is left alone.</p><p>Five groups around one carbon is crowded. If {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}} are hydrogens there is room. If they are carbon groups, the nucleophile cannot reach the back of the carbon, and the transition state is too high in energy. That is why the order of reactivity is methyl, then primary, then secondary, and tertiary does not react by {{SN2}} at all.</p><p>Why must the two groups be 180° apart? The nucleophile gives electrons to the antibonding orbital, whose big lobe is on the side of the carbon opposite the leaving group. Attack there gives the best overlap and keeps the two electron-rich groups as far apart as possible.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>In {{SN2}}, the nucleophile attacks the carbon from the side opposite the leaving group, so the other three groups flip like an umbrella in the wind. This flip is <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>The second feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom, the carbon that the nucleophile attacks. The middle moment of the reaction is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>, where Nu is the nucleophile and LG is the leaving group.</p><p>The nucleophile is on one side of the carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. Both are held by partial bonds, shown as dots. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie in one plane through the carbon. For an instant the carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>: it has three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups through the reaction. In the starting material they lean away from the leaving group, toward the side the nucleophile will come from. In the transition state they are flat. In the product they lean away from the nucleophile, toward the side the leaving group left. They have turned inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>If the nucleophile has a negative charge, the charge is shared in the transition state, partly on the nucleophile and partly on the leaving group. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group is inverted, and other stereocentres in the molecule stay as they were.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>The second feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom, the atom the nucleophile attacks. Inversion means that the three groups on that carbon that do not take part turn inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind. The middle moment of the reaction, the transition state, is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>. Nu is the nucleophile, LG is the leaving group, and the dots are partial bonds: bonds that are partly made or partly broken.</p><p>The nucleophile is on one side of the carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie in one plane through the carbon: {{R1}} straight up, {{R2}} on a dash and {{R3}} on a wedge. For an instant carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>, meaning it is joined to five things: three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups through the reaction. In the starting material they lean away from the leaving group, toward the side the nucleophile will come from. In the transition state they are flat. In the product they lean away from the nucleophile, toward the side the leaving group left. That is the umbrella turning inside out.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>The shape of the carbon changes in a regular way. At the start it is {{sp3}} and tetrahedral, with bond angles near 109.5°. In the transition state the three groups that stay are in a plane with 120° between them, and the nucleophile and leaving group sit above and below that plane on a straight line. This shape is called trigonal bipyramidal, and the carbon is close to {{sp2}}, with its p orbital shared between the nucleophile and the leaving group. At the end it is {{sp3}} and tetrahedral again, pointing the other way.</p><p>With a nucleophile that carries a negative charge, the charge is shared in the transition state: part on the nucleophile and part on the leaving group. So for {{HO-}} reacting with {{CH3I}}, the transition state has O and I on opposite sides of the carbon, each joined by a dotted bond, with a partial negative charge, δ-, on both, and the three hydrogens in one plane.</p><p>Why does the attack come from the back? The nucleophile has to put its lone pair into an empty orbital on the electrophile. The lowest empty orbital of the carbon-leaving group bond is its antibonding orbital, and its large lobe points out from the back of the carbon, away from the leaving group. So the best overlap is from the back. Putting electrons into this orbital also weakens the bond to the leaving group, which is why that bond breaks as the new one forms. A front attack would put the nucleophile in a node, right next to the electron-rich leaving group.</p><p>Only the carbon that carried the leaving group is inverted. Any other stereocentre in the molecule is left alone, because {{SN2}} touches one carbon only.</p><p>Five groups around one carbon is crowded. If {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}} are hydrogens there is room. If they are carbon groups, the nucleophile cannot reach the back of the carbon, and the transition state is too high in energy. That is why the order of reactivity is methyl, then primary, then secondary, with tertiary not reacting by {{SN2}} at all.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>In {{SN2}} the nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon from the side opposite the leaving group, so the carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b> in the transition state and its other three groups invert like an umbrella in the wind.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>The second feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom. The transition state is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>.</p><p>The nucleophile is on one side of carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. Both are held by partial bonds, shown as dots. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie in one plane through the carbon: {{R1}} straight up, {{R2}} on a dash and {{R3}} on a wedge. For an instant carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>: three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups. In the starting material they lean away from the leaving group, toward the side the nucleophile will come from. In the transition state they are flat. In the product they lean away from the nucleophile, toward the side the leaving group left. They have turned inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind. That is inversion.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>With a negatively charged nucleophile, the charge is shared in the transition state: part on the nucleophile and part on the leaving group. Only the carbon that held the leaving group inverts; any other stereocentre in the molecule keeps its configuration.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>The second feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom. The transition state is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>, with dots for partial bonds.</p><p>The nucleophile is on one side of carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. Both are held by partial bonds. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie in one plane through the carbon: {{R1}} straight up, {{R2}} on a dash and {{R3}} on a wedge. For an instant carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>: three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups through the reaction. In the starting material they lean away from the leaving group, toward the side the nucleophile will come from. In the transition state they are flat. In the product they lean away from the nucleophile, toward the side the leaving group left. They have turned inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind. That is inversion.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>The geometry at carbon goes like this. At the start it is {{sp3}} and tetrahedral, with angles near 109.5°. In the transition state the three groups that stay are in a plane with 120° between them, and the nucleophile and leaving group sit above and below that plane on a straight line. This shape is called trigonal bipyramidal, and the carbon is close to {{sp2}}, with its p orbital shared between the nucleophile and the leaving group. At the end it is {{sp3}} and tetrahedral again, pointing the other way.</p><p>With a nucleophile that carries a negative charge, the charge is shared in the transition state: part on the nucleophile and part on the leaving group. For {{HO-}} with {{CH3I}}, that means O and I on opposite sides of the carbon, each joined by a dotted bond, with δ- on both, and the three hydrogens in one plane.</p><p>The reason for the backside attack is orbital overlap. The nucleophile's lone pair has to go into an empty orbital on the electrophile. The lowest empty orbital of a carbon-leaving group bond is its antibonding orbital, σ*, and its large lobe points out from the back of the carbon, away from the leaving group. Overlap is best there. Putting electrons into σ* also weakens the bond to the leaving group, so that bond breaks as the new one forms. Attack from the front would put the nucleophile in a node and next to the electron-rich leaving group. This also answers why the nucleophile and the leaving group must be 180° apart: that arrangement gives the best overlap and keeps the two electron-rich groups as far apart as possible.</p><p>Only the carbon that carried the leaving group inverts. Another stereocentre in the molecule is untouched, because {{SN2}} acts on one carbon only. If you are given a chiral substrate, invert that one centre and copy the rest.</p><p>Five groups around one carbon is crowded. If {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}} are hydrogens there is room. If they are carbon groups, the nucleophile cannot reach the back of the carbon, and the transition state is too high in energy. That is why the order of reactivity is methyl, then primary, then secondary, with tertiary not reacting by {{SN2}} at all. Questions 3C and 5A of the past exam both rest on this picture.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>{{SN2}} proceeds with <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic carbon: the nucleophile attacks 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b> in which the other three groups are flat.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>The second numbered feature of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom. The transition state is drawn <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>. The nucleophile is on one side of carbon and the leaving group is directly opposite, 180° away. Both are held by partial bonds, shown as dots. The other three groups, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, lie in one plane through the carbon: {{R1}} straight up, {{R2}} on a dash and {{R3}} on a wedge. For an instant carbon is <b>five-coordinate</b>: three ordinary bonds and two half bonds.</p><p>Follow the three groups through the reaction. In the starting material they lean away from the leaving group, toward the side the nucleophile will come from. In the transition state they are flat. In the product they lean away from the nucleophile, toward the side the leaving group left. They have turned inside out, like an umbrella in a strong wind. That is inversion.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>With a nucleophile that carries a negative charge, the charge is shared in the transition state: part on the nucleophile and part on the leaving group. For {{HO-}} reacting with {{CH3I}}, the transition state has O and I on opposite sides of the carbon, each joined by a dotted bond, with δ- on both, and the three hydrogens in one plane.</p><p>The geometry at carbon runs from {{sp3}} to {{sp3}} through a flattened middle. Start: tetrahedral, angles near 109.5°. Transition state: the three groups that stay are in a plane with 120° between them, and Nu and LG sit above and below that plane on a straight line. This shape is trigonal bipyramidal, and the carbon is close to {{sp2}} with its p orbital shared between Nu and LG. End: {{sp3}} and tetrahedral again, pointing the other way.</p><p>The orbital reason for backside attack: the nucleophile's lone pair has to go into an empty orbital on the electrophile. The lowest empty orbital of a C-LG bond is its antibonding orbital, σ*, and the large lobe of σ* points out from the back of the carbon, away from the leaving group. So the best overlap is from the back. Putting electrons into σ* also weakens the C-LG bond, which is why that bond breaks as the new one forms. Attack from the front would put the nucleophile in a node and next to the electron-rich leaving group. This is also the answer to why Nu and LG must be 180° apart: the best overlap and the largest separation of the two electron-rich groups both occur there.</p><p>Only the carbon that carried the leaving group is inverted. Other stereocentres in the molecule keep their configuration, because {{SN2}} touches one carbon only. For a substrate such as (2S,3S)-2-bromo-3-methylpentane reacting with NaSH, invert C2 and leave C3 as it was.</p><p>The crowded transition state explains the next lecture. Five groups around one carbon is crowded. If {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}} are hydrogens there is room. If they are carbon groups, the nucleophile cannot reach the back of the carbon, and the transition state is too high in energy. That is why the order of reactivity is methyl, then primary, then secondary, with tertiary not reacting by {{SN2}} at all. Questions 3C and 5A of the past exam both rest on this picture.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>Nucleophilic attack in {{SN2}} occurs along the axis of the carbon-leaving group bond, opposite the leaving group, so configuration at that carbon is inverted through a trigonal bipyramidal, <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>The second characteristic of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> of configuration at the electrophilic atom, a stereospecific outcome of backside attack. The transition state is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>: nucleophile and leaving group are collinear with carbon, 180° apart, each held by a partial bond. The other three substituents, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, are coplanar with the carbon. The carbon is formally <b>five-coordinate</b>, with three full bonds and two partial ones.</p><p>The substituents are displaced as a set. They pyramidalize away from the leaving group in the substrate, pass through planarity at the transition state, and pyramidalize away from the nucleophile in the product, like an umbrella blown inside out.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>The geometry is trigonal bipyramidal, with the nucleophile and the leaving group apical and the carbon close to {{sp2}} hybridized, its p orbital shared by Nu and LG. For an anionic nucleophile the negative charge is delocalized over both: it is partly on the nucleophile and partly on the leaving group. Inversion occurs at the reacting carbon only. Other stereocentres are not affected, so {{SN2}} on a chiral substrate is stereospecific.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The second characteristic of {{SN2}} is <b>inversion</b> of configuration at the electrophilic atom, a stereospecific consequence of backside attack. The transition state is written <b>[Nu···C···LG] ‡</b>. The nucleophile and the leaving group are collinear with carbon, 180° apart, each held by a partial bond. The other three substituents, {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}}, are coplanar with the carbon. The carbon is formally <b>five-coordinate</b>, with three full bonds and two partial ones.</p><p>The three substituents move as a set. They pyramidalize away from the leaving group in the substrate, pass through planarity at the transition state, and pyramidalize away from the nucleophile in the product, like an umbrella blown inside out.</p>
[[rxn:C[C@@H]([2H])Cl + [OH-]>>C[C@H]([2H])O + [Cl-]]]
<p>The geometry along the path runs from {{sp3}} to {{sp3}} through a flattened middle. In the substrate the carbon is tetrahedral and the leaving group is one of four ligands, with angles near 109.5°. At the transition state the three spectator groups lie in a plane with 120° between them, and the nucleophile and the leaving group occupy the apical positions of a trigonal bipyramid. The carbon is close to {{sp2}}, with its p orbital shared between Nu and LG, so the Nu-C-LG unit is a three-center, four-electron arrangement. For an anionic nucleophile the negative charge is delocalized over both ends: part on the nucleophile and part on the leaving group.</p><p>The stereoelectronic reason for backside attack is frontier orbital overlap. The nucleophile donates its highest occupied orbital, here a lone pair, into the lowest unoccupied orbital of the electrophile. For a carbon-leaving group bond that is the antibonding orbital σ*, and its large lobe points out from the back of the carbon, away from the leaving group. Overlap is therefore best from the back. Populating σ* also weakens the C-LG bond, so bond breaking is coupled to bond making. Front attack would place the nucleophile at a node of σ* and next to the electron-rich leaving group, giving poor overlap and electrostatic repulsion. The same two factors, overlap and repulsion, fix the 180° angle of the nucleophile, carbon and leaving group.</p><p>Inversion is local. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group is inverted, and any other stereocentre in the molecule keeps its configuration, so the reaction is stereospecific: one enantiomer of a chiral substrate gives one enantiomer of the product, not a mixture. If the carbon is not a stereocentre, as in a {{CH2}} or {{CH3}} group, inversion still occurs but there is nothing to observe. Whether the R or S label changes depends on how the priorities of the groups rank before and after substitution. For (2S,3S)-2-bromo-3-methylpentane with NaSH, C2 is inverted and C3 is untouched.</p><p>The crowding of the five-coordinate transition state also explains the pattern of reactivity. If {{R1}}, {{R2}} and {{R3}} are hydrogens there is room. If they are carbon groups, the nucleophile cannot reach the back of the carbon, and the transition state is raised too high in energy. The order of reactivity is therefore methyl, then primary, then secondary, and tertiary does not react by {{SN2}} at all. The five-coordinate geometry is that of a saddle point, a maximum along the reaction coordinate, and not that of a species that can be isolated. Questions 3C and 5A of the past exam both rest on this picture.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 7 · The three facts of SN2, and Walden inversion on a ring
  6: {
    "1.1": `<p>This reaction follows three facts. Its speed depends on two molecules. It happens all in one step. The carbon flips over like an umbrella in the wind, which is called <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>This kind of reaction follows three facts. Learn them as a set.</p><ol><li>How fast it goes depends on two things: the new group coming in and the molecule it attacks. More of either one makes it faster.</li><li>Everything happens at once, in a single step, with no stopping place in the middle.</li><li>The carbon flips over, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>.</li></ol><p>Here is an example where the flip is easy to see. Picture a ring molecule. One carbon in the ring has an iodine atom that points toward you. A new group, SH, takes the place of the iodine.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>The SH cannot come from the front, because the iodine is there. It comes from behind the ring. So the new bond points away from you. The iodine pointed toward you, and the SH points away from you. That is the flip.</p><p>Only that one carbon changes. Every other atom in the ring stays exactly where it was.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>This kind of reaction follows three facts. Learn them as a set.</p><ol><li>How fast it goes depends on two things: the new group that comes in and the molecule it attacks. If you have more of either one, the reaction goes faster.</li><li>Everything happens at once, in a single step. There is no stopping place in the middle.</li><li>The carbon that is attacked flips over, like an umbrella turned inside out by the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>.</li></ol><p>Now an example where the flip is easy to see. The molecule is called 3-iodocyclohexanone. It is a ring. One carbon in the ring holds an iodine atom. In a drawing, the iodine is on a wedge. A wedge means that the atom points toward you, out of the page.</p><p>The molecule meets sodium hydrosulfide, which is made of {{Na+}} and {{-SH}}. The SH part is the new group. It takes the place of the iodine, and the iodine leaves.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Where does the new group come in? It cannot come in from the front, because the iodine is there. It comes in from behind the ring, from the side opposite the iodine. So the new bond points away from you. In a drawing, a bond that points away from you is drawn as a dash. The iodine was on a wedge, so the SH ends up on a dash. That is the flip. Chemists also call it Walden inversion, which is the old name for the flip that goes with this reaction.</p><p>The sodium is only a bystander. It starts out next to the SH part and ends up next to the iodine part. It does not take part in the change.</p><p>Only one carbon changes: the carbon that held the iodine. Every other atom in the ring stays exactly where it was. The C=O part of the ring does not move at all.</p><p>Here is a quick way to draw the product. First, find the carbon that holds the leaving group. Second, take the leaving group off and put the new group on that same carbon. Third, if the leaving group was on a wedge, draw the new bond as a dash. If it was on a dash, draw a wedge. Fourth, leave every other part of the molecule as it was.</p><p>Why does the SH end up on a dash? The new group must come in from the side opposite the group that leaves. The iodine was on the face toward you. So the new group came from the far face, and its bond points away from you.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>{{SN2}} follows three facts: it is <b>second order</b>, it is <b>concerted</b> in one step, and it goes with <b>inversion</b>, the carbon flipping like an umbrella in the wind.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>The three facts of {{SN2}} go together. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: the speed depends on both reactants, rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}].</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: it happens in one step, with no intermediates.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom: the carbon flips over.</li></ol><p>Here is an example where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone has its iodine on a wedge, pointing toward you. It reacts with NaSH. In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>, pointing away from you. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring. The flip is called <b>Walden inversion</b>.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>The sodium only watches. Only the carbon that held the iodine changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, stays where it was. Other groups on the ring keep their places too.</p>`,
    "2.3": `<p>The three facts of {{SN2}} go together. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: the speed depends on both reactants, rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}]. Making either one more concentrated makes the reaction faster.</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: it happens all at once in one step, with no intermediates. Nothing waits in the middle.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom: the carbon that is attacked flips over, like an umbrella in the wind.</li></ol><p>Here is an example where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone has its iodine on a wedge. A wedge means the iodine points toward you. It reacts with NaSH, made of {{Na+}} and {{-SH}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>. A dash means the bond points away from you. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring, and the new C-S bond points away. The flip has a name: <b>Walden inversion</b>. It is the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p><p>Sodium is a spectator. It starts as the partner of {{HS-}} and ends as the partner of {{I-}}. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, is exactly where it was. If the ring had another group somewhere else, it would keep its place too.</p><p>To draw a product like this, follow four steps. Find the carbon with the leaving group. Take the leaving group off and put the nucleophile on the same carbon. If the leaving group was on a wedge, draw the new bond as a dash, and the other way round. Leave everything else alone, because {{SN2}} changes only one carbon.</p><p>Why does the SH end up on a dash? The nucleophile must attack from the side opposite the leaving group. The iodine was on the face toward you, so {{HS-}} came in from the far face. Because the attack always comes from one face, the result is one product, not a mixture.</p><p>Each fact is a different kind of question. Fact 1 gives rate questions, such as which reaction is faster or which molecule reacts fastest. Fact 2 gives diagram and mechanism questions. Fact 3 gives stereochemistry questions, where the product has to be drawn inverted. Later you will compare each new reaction with these three lines. {{SN1}} is first order, goes in steps and gives a mixture of mirror images. {{E2}} is second order and concerted, like {{SN2}}.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>{{SN2}} has three facts to learn together: it is <b>second order</b>, it is <b>concerted</b> (one step, no intermediates), and it goes with <b>inversion</b> at the carbon that is attacked.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>The next lecture begins by collecting everything into three facts. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: the speed depends on the concentration of both reactants, rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}].</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: it happens in one step, with no intermediates.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom: the three other groups on that carbon flip over.</li></ol><p>Then an example where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge (pointing toward you), reacts with NaSH, made of {{Na+}} and {{-SH}}. In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>, which means it points away from you. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring. The lecture calls this <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator: it starts with {{HS-}} and ends with {{I-}}. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, stays where it was.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>The next lecture begins by collecting everything into three facts about {{SN2}}. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: the speed depends on the concentration of both reactants, rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}]. Both the nucleophile and the electrophile are in the slow step, so changing either one changes the rate.</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: the reaction happens in one step, with no intermediates. The new bond forms as the old bond breaks.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom: the nucleophile attacks from the side opposite the leaving group, so the three other groups on that carbon flip over, like an umbrella in the wind.</li></ol><p>Then an example of fact 3 where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge, reacts with NaSH ({{Na+}} and {{-SH}}). A wedge is a bond drawn pointing toward you, out of the page. In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>, a bond drawn pointing away from you, into the page. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring, and the new C-S bond points away from you. The lecture calls this <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator. It starts as the partner of {{HS-}} and ends as the partner of {{I-}}. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, is exactly where it was.</p><p>You can draw this kind of product in four steps. Step 1: find the carbon bonded to the leaving group. Step 2: erase the leaving group and put the nucleophile on the same carbon. Step 3: if the leaving group was on a wedge, draw the new bond as a dash, and the reverse. Step 4: leave every other stereocentre alone, because {{SN2}} touches one carbon only. If the carbon is not a stereocentre (a {{CH2}} or {{CH3}}) inversion still happens, but there is nothing to show.</p><p>Why does the SH end up on a dash when the iodine was on a wedge? The nucleophile must attack the carbon from the side opposite the leaving group. Iodine was on the face toward the viewer, so {{HS-}} approached from the far face.</p><p>Each fact is a way to ask about {{SN2}}. Fact 1 gives rate questions: which reaction is faster, or which substrate reacts fastest. Fact 2 gives diagram and mechanism questions. Fact 3 gives stereochemistry questions: the product is drawn inverted. In the chapters that follow, compare each new reaction with these three lines. {{SN1}} is first order, goes in steps and gives a mixture of both mirror images. {{E2}} is second order and concerted like {{SN2}}.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is <b>second order</b>, <b>concerted</b> (no intermediates) and proceeds with <b>inversion</b> at the electrophile atom: the nucleophile ends up on the face opposite where the leaving group was.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>The next lecture begins by collecting everything into three facts. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}].</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: no intermediates.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom.</li></ol><p>Then an example of fact 3 where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge, reacts with NaSH ({{Na+}} and {{-SH}}). In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring, and the new C-S bond points away from you. The lecture labels this <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator. It starts as the partner of {{HS-}} and ends as the partner of {{I-}}. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, is exactly where it was, and any other stereocentre keeps its configuration.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>The next lecture begins by collecting everything into three facts about {{SN2}}. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}]. Both reactants are in the rate-determining step, so the rate is first order in each and second order overall.</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: no intermediates. Bond making and bond breaking happen in one step.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom, because the nucleophile attacks from the side opposite the leaving group.</li></ol><p>Then an example of fact 3 where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge, reacts with NaSH ({{Na+}} and {{-SH}}). In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring, and the new C-S bond points away from you. The lecture labels this <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator. It starts as the partner of {{HS-}} and ends as the partner of {{I-}}. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, is exactly where it was.</p><p>A quick way to draw the product follows from this. Step 1: find the carbon bonded to the leaving group. Step 2: erase the leaving group and put the nucleophile on the same carbon. Step 3: if the leaving group was on a wedge, draw the new bond as a dash, and the reverse. Step 4: leave every other stereocentre alone, because {{SN2}} touches one carbon only. If the carbon is not a stereocentre (a {{CH2}} or {{CH3}}) inversion still happens, but there is nothing to show.</p><p>Suppose a substituted ring has the leaving group on a wedge and a methyl group on a dash somewhere else. The product has the nucleophile on a dash, and the methyl group is still on a dash. It is one stereoisomer, not a mixture of both faces, because the attack comes from one face only.</p><p>Each fact is a way to ask about {{SN2}}. Fact 1 gives rate questions: which reaction is faster, which substrate reacts fastest. Fact 2 gives diagram and mechanism questions. Fact 3 gives stereochemistry questions: the product is drawn inverted. In the chapters that follow, compare each new reaction with these three lines: {{SN1}} is first order, stepwise and racemizes; {{E2}} is second order and concerted like {{SN2}}.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is summed up by three facts: <b>second order</b>, <b>concerted</b> and <b>inversion</b> at the electrophile atom, as when the iodine on a wedge in 3-iodocyclohexanone is replaced by SH on a <b>dash</b>.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>The next lecture begins by collecting everything into three lines. Learn them as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}].</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: no intermediates.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophile atom.</li></ol><p>Then an example of fact 3 where the flip is easy to see. 3-Iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge, reacts with NaSH ({{Na+}} and {{-SH}}). In the product the SH is on a <b>dash</b>. The iodine pointed toward you, so the nucleophile came from behind the ring, and the new C-S bond points away from you. The notes label this <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator. It starts as the partner of {{HS-}} and ends as the partner of {{I-}}. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes. Every other atom in the ring, including the C=O, is exactly where it was.</p><p>A quick way to draw the product: Step 1, find the carbon bonded to the leaving group. Step 2, erase the leaving group and put the nucleophile on the same carbon. Step 3, if the leaving group was on a wedge, draw the new bond as a dash, and the reverse. Step 4, leave every other stereocentre alone, because {{SN2}} touches one carbon only. If the carbon is not a stereocentre (a {{CH2}} or {{CH3}}) inversion still happens, but there is nothing to show. For trans-1-bromo-4-methylcyclohexane with NaCN, put CN on the opposite face from the bromine and leave the methyl group where it is. The product is one stereoisomer, not a mixture, because the attack comes from one face only.</p><p>Why does the SH end up on a dash when the iodine was on a wedge? The nucleophile must attack the carbon from the side opposite the leaving group. Iodine was on the face toward the viewer, so {{HS-}} approached from the far face and its bond points away from the viewer.</p><p>Each fact is a way to ask about {{SN2}}. Fact 1 gives rate questions: which reaction is faster (Question 2A), which substrate reacts fastest (Question 3C). Fact 2 gives diagram and mechanism questions (Questions 3B, 4B and 5A). Fact 3 gives stereochemistry questions: the product in Question 2A is drawn inverted. In the chapters that follow, compare each new reaction with these three lines: {{SN1}} is first order, stepwise and racemizes; {{E2}} is second order and concerted like {{SN2}}.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>The {{SN2}} signature is a second-order rate law, a single concerted step without intermediates, and stereospecific <b>inversion</b> at the electrophilic carbon (<b>Walden inversion</b>).</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>The three characteristics of {{SN2}} are best learned as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}], first order in each reactant, as expected when both are in the single bimolecular step.</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: no intermediates, one transition state.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom, a stereospecific result of backside attack.</li></ol><p>An example in which the inversion is easy to see: 3-iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge, reacts with NaSH ({{Na+}} and {{-SH}}), and the SH appears on a <b>dash</b>. The iodine pointed toward the viewer, so the nucleophile attacked the carbon from the face opposite the leaving group, and the new C-S bond points away. This is <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator ion, paired with {{HS-}} at the start and with {{I-}} at the end. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes configuration. Every other atom, including the carbonyl, is unchanged, as is any other stereocentre.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The three characteristics of {{SN2}} are best learned as a set:</p><ol><li><b>Second order</b>: rate = <i>k</i>[Nu:<sup>−</sup>][{{E+}}], first order in each reactant and second order overall. For an elementary step the order equals the molecularity, and here both the nucleophile and the electrophile are in the one bimolecular step.</li><li><b>Concerted</b>: no intermediates, so there is a single transition state on the path from reactants to products.</li><li><b>Inversion</b> at the electrophilic atom, a stereospecific result of backside attack on the carbon.</li></ol><p>The three observations support one another. A second-order law alone does not identify the mechanism, since {{E2}} is also second order and concerted. It is the combination of the rate law, the absence of intermediates and the inverted stereochemistry that identifies {{SN2}}.</p><p>An example in which the inversion is easy to see: 3-iodocyclohexanone, with the iodine on a wedge, reacts with NaSH ({{Na+}} and {{-SH}}), and the SH appears on a <b>dash</b>. The iodine pointed toward the viewer, so the nucleophile attacked the carbon from the face opposite the leaving group, and the new C-S bond points away. This is <b>Walden inversion</b>, the historical name for the inversion that goes with {{SN2}}, after Paul Walden, who first observed that a substitution can invert the configuration of a stereocentre.</p>
[[rxn:O=C1C[C@H](I)CCC1 + [Na+].[SH-]>>O=C1C[C@@H](S)CCC1 + [Na+].[I-]]]
<p>Sodium is a spectator ion, paired with {{HS-}} at the start and with {{I-}} at the end. Only the carbon that carried the leaving group changes configuration. Every other atom, including the carbonyl, is exactly where it was, and any other stereocentre keeps its configuration because the reaction acts at one carbon only.</p><p>To predict a product: locate the carbon bonded to the leaving group, replace the leaving group with the nucleophile on the opposite face, so that a wedge becomes a dash and the reverse, and leave all other stereocentres alone. If that carbon is not a stereocentre, as in a {{CH2}} or {{CH3}} group, inversion still occurs but cannot be seen. On a ring, a trans substrate such as trans-1-bromo-4-methylcyclohexane gives the product with the nucleophile on the face opposite the bromine and the methyl group unchanged. The result is a single stereoisomer, not an equal mixture of both faces, which would signal a planar intermediate.</p><p>Each fact corresponds to a kind of question. Fact 1 gives rate questions: which reaction is faster (Question 2A), which substrate reacts fastest (Question 3C). Fact 2 gives diagram and mechanism questions (Questions 3B, 4B and 5A). Fact 3 gives stereochemistry questions: the product in Question 2A is drawn inverted. In the chapters that follow, each new reaction is compared with these three lines: {{SN1}} is first order, stepwise and racemizes; {{E2}} is second order and concerted like {{SN2}}.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 8 · Homework from the lecture
  7: {
    "1.1": `<p>Chloride is a good <b>leaving group</b>, and hydroxide is a poor one. That is why the reaction runs forward and does not run back.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>In this reaction, hydroxide pushes chloride off a carbon. Why does it not run backward, with chloride pushing hydroxide off?</p><p>A group that leaves well is calm when it holds its pair of electrons alone. Chloride is calm. It is a <b>weak base</b>. Hydroxide is not calm. It is a <b>strong base</b>, so it leaves badly.</p><p>Going forward, a strong base is used up and a weak base is made. That is downhill, like a ball rolling down a hill. Going backward would be uphill, and a very long way. So the reaction goes forward and does not go back.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>Here is a reaction. A hydroxide group pushes a chloride group off a carbon and takes its place. The question is why it does not run the other way.</p><p>Think about what the other way would look like. Chloride would come at the carbon of the new molecule and push hydroxide off. In that case chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b>, the one that attacks. Hydroxide would be the <b>leaving group</b>, the one that is pushed out and takes its pair of electrons with it.</p><p>So the real question is this: which group is better at leaving?</p><p>A good leaving group is calm. It is happy to carry the pair of electrons by itself. A group that is happy like that is a <b>weak base</b>. A group that is not happy carrying the pair is a <b>strong base</b>, and it is a poor leaving group.</p><p>Chloride is a very weak base. It is comfortable alone with its electrons. Hydroxide is a strong base. It is not comfortable, and it does not leave well.</p><p>Now compare the two directions:</p><ul><li><strong>Forward.</strong> A strong base is used up and a weak base is made. That is downhill, like a ball rolling down a hill.</li><li><strong>Backward.</strong> A weak base would have to push out a strong base. That is uphill, and a very long way, like pushing the ball back up.</li></ul><p>The lecture shows the same thing with ethanol and a group written {{-SH}}. Nothing happens, because hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</p><p>So the molecules at the end are much calmer than the molecules at the start. The reaction goes forward, and chloride cannot push hydroxide off.</p>`,
    "2.1": `<p>The reverse reaction does not happen because hydroxide is a strong base and a poor <b>leaving group</b>, while chloride is a weak base and a good one.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>The reaction is {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}. Why does it not run in reverse?</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> {{Cl-}} would attack the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and push out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b>, and hydroxide would be the <b>leaving group</b>.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>, because it is calm holding the electron pair. Each base has a partner acid with a number called {{pKa}}. For chloride the partner is HCl, about −7, so chloride is a very weak base. For hydroxide the partner is water, 15.7, so hydroxide is a <b>strong base</b>.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Forward, a strong base is used up and a weak base is made: downhill. In reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base: uphill, by a lot.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> {{CH3CH2OH}} with {{-SH}} shows the same thing: no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol>`,
    "2.3": `<p>Here is the reaction: {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}. Hydroxide attacks the carbon and chloride leaves. The question is why it does not run in reverse.</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> {{Cl-}} would attack the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and push out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b>, the attacker. Hydroxide would be the <b>leaving group</b>, the one pushed out with its electron pair.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>. That means it is stable, or calm, when it holds the electron pair by itself. To compare bases, look at the partner acid of each and its {{pKa}}. HCl has a {{pKa}} of about −7, so chloride is a very weak base. Water has a {{pKa}} of 15.7, so hydroxide is a <b>strong base</b>.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Going forward, a strong base ({{HO-}}) is used up and a weak base ({{Cl-}}) is made. That is downhill, like a ball rolling down. Going in reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base. That is uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> The notes of 11/05 show the same thing with {{CH3CH2OH}} and {{-SH}}. There is no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>The answer: hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, and chloride is a weak base and a good one. Methanol and chloride are much calmer than the starting molecules, so the reaction goes far to the right and chloride cannot push hydroxide off methanol.</p>`,
    "3.1": `<p>Hydroxide is a strong base and a poor <b>leaving group</b>, while chloride is a weak base and a good one, so the products sit far downhill and the reverse reaction does not happen.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>The reaction is {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}. Why does the reverse not happen?</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> {{Cl-}} would attack the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and push out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b> and hydroxide the <b>leaving group</b>.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>: it is stable holding the electron pair. Check the {{pKa}} of the <b>conjugate acid</b> of each. HCl is about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base. Water is 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Forward, a strong base is used up and a weak base is released: downhill. In reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base: uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> {{CH3CH2OH}} with {{-SH}} gives no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>So the equilibrium lies far to the right, and chloride cannot displace hydroxide from methanol.</p>`,
    "3.3": `<p>The forward reaction is {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}. It works well. The question is why the reverse does not happen.</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> The reverse would be {{Cl-}} attacking the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and pushing out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b>, the Lewis base that attacks carbon. Hydroxide would be the <b>leaving group</b>, which departs with the bonding pair of electrons.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>, which means it is stable holding the electron pair. To rank bases, look at the {{pKa}} of the <b>conjugate acid</b>, the acid you get by adding a proton. The conjugate acid of chloride is HCl, with a {{pKa}} of about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base. The conjugate acid of hydroxide is water, with a {{pKa}} of 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>. The lower the {{pKa}} of the conjugate acid, the weaker the base.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Going forward, a strong base ({{HO-}}) is used up and a weak base ({{Cl-}}) is released. That is downhill: the products are more stable than the reactants. Going in reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base. That is uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> The 11/05 notes show the same thing with {{CH3CH2OH}} and {{-SH}}. There is no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>The answer: hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, while chloride is a weak base and a good leaving group. Methanol and chloride are much more stable than the reactants, so the equilibrium lies far to the right. Chloride cannot displace hydroxide from methanol.</p>`,
    "4.1": `<p>The reverse does not happen because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor <b>leaving group</b>, while {{Cl-}} is a weak base and a good one, so the forward reaction is strongly downhill.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>For {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}, why does the reverse not occur?</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> {{Cl-}} would attack the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and push out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b> and hydroxide the <b>leaving group</b>.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>, stable holding the electron pair. Use the {{pKa}} of the <b>conjugate acid</b>: HCl is about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base; water is 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Forward, a strong base is used up and a weak base is released: downhill. In reverse, a weak base would have to expel a strong base: uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> {{CH3CH2OH}} with {{-SH}} gives no reaction for the same reason: {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>The equilibrium lies far to the right.</p>`,
    "4.3": `<p>The forward reaction is {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}. The question is why the reverse does not occur.</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> The reverse would be {{Cl-}} attacking the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and pushing out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b> and hydroxide the <b>leaving group</b>: it would depart with the bonding pair of electrons.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>: it is stable holding the electron pair. The {{pKa}} of the <b>conjugate acid</b> measures this. HCl is about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base. Water is 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>. The stronger the acid, the weaker its conjugate base, and the better that base leaves.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Forward, a strong base ({{HO-}}) is used up and a weak base ({{Cl-}}) is released: downhill, to more stable products. In reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base: uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> The 11/05 notes show the same thing with {{CH3CH2OH}} and {{-SH}}: no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>So hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, while chloride is a weak base and a good one. Methanol and chloride are much more stable than the reactants, the equilibrium lies far to the right, and chloride cannot displace hydroxide from methanol. This is why a good leaving group matters in an {{SN2}} reaction: the more stable the group is holding the pair, the more easily the substitution goes.</p>`,
    "5.1": `<p>The reverse of {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} does not occur because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor <b>leaving group</b>, while {{Cl-}} is a weak base and a good one: the equilibrium lies far to the right.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>Why does the reverse of {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}} not occur?</p><ol><li><strong>Write the reverse.</strong> The reverse would be {{Cl-}} attacking the carbon of {{CH3OH}} and pushing out {{HO-}}. Chloride would be the <b>nucleophile</b> and hydroxide the <b>leaving group</b>.</li><li><strong>Judge the leaving group.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>: it is stable holding the electron pair. Find the {{pKa}} of each <b>conjugate acid</b>. HCl is about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base. Water is 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>. A lower {{pKa}} of the conjugate acid means a weaker base and a better leaving group.</li><li><strong>Compare the two sides.</strong> Forward, a strong base ({{HO-}}) is used up and a weak base ({{Cl-}}) is released: downhill. In reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base: uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>Connect to the lecture.</strong> The 11/05 notes show the same thing with {{CH3CH2OH}} and {{-SH}}: no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>This is the same reasoning used for acid and base questions: the equilibrium favors the side with the more stable, weaker base. Here the substitution goes in the direction that releases the better leaving group.</p><p>Answer: hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, while chloride is a weak base and a good leaving group. The products (methanol and chloride) are much more stable than the reactants, so the equilibrium lies far to the right and chloride cannot displace hydroxide from methanol.</p>`,
    "6.1": `<p>The reverse reaction is not observed because {{HO-}}, the conjugate base of a weak acid ({{pKa}} 15.7), is a strong base and a poor <b>leaving group</b>, while {{Cl-}} (conjugate acid {{pKa}} about −7) is a weak base and a good one; the equilibrium lies far to the right.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>For {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}, the reverse is a substitution with the roles exchanged, and the thermodynamics decide against it.</p><ol><li><strong>The reverse reaction.</strong> {{Cl-}} would act as the <b>nucleophile</b> at the carbon of {{CH3OH}}, and {{HO-}} would have to depart as the <b>leaving group</b>.</li><li><strong>Leaving group ability.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>, stable as the free anion holding the electron pair. The {{pKa}} of the <b>conjugate acid</b> is the usual guide: HCl is about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base; water is 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>. The correlation is good but approximate, because solvent and other effects also play a part.</li><li><strong>The energetics.</strong> Forward, a strong base is consumed and a weak base is released, so the reaction is strongly downhill and the equilibrium constant is large. In reverse, a weak base would have to expel a strong base: uphill, by a large amount.</li><li><strong>The lecture's second case.</strong> {{CH3CH2OH}} with {{-SH}} gives no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>Chloride cannot displace hydroxide from methanol.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The forward reaction is {{HO-}} + {{H3C-Cl}} giving {{HO-CH3}} + {{Cl-}}. The reverse is also a substitution of the same kind, with the roles of the two anions exchanged. That it is not observed is a statement about thermodynamics.</p><ol><li><strong>The reverse reaction.</strong> {{Cl-}} would be the <b>nucleophile</b>, attacking the carbon of {{CH3OH}}, and {{HO-}} would be the <b>leaving group</b>, departing with the bonding pair of electrons.</li><li><strong>Leaving group ability.</strong> A good leaving group is a <b>weak base</b>: the anion is stable holding the electron pair, so little is lost when the bond to carbon breaks. The standard guide is the {{pKa}} of the <b>conjugate acid</b>. HCl is about −7, so {{Cl-}} is a very weak base. Water is 15.7, so {{HO-}} is a <b>strong base</b>. This is a correlation between acidity of the conjugate acid and leaving group ability. It is reliable for comparisons like this one, but it is not exact, since solvation and the nature of the bond being broken also matter.</li><li><strong>The energetics.</strong> Forward, a strong base ({{HO-}}) is consumed and a weak base ({{Cl-}}) is released: downhill. The free energy change is strongly favorable and the equilibrium constant is large. In reverse, a weak base would have to push out a strong base: uphill, by a large amount. A reaction this unfavorable overall is not seen in that direction.</li><li><strong>The lecture's second case.</strong> The 11/05 notes show the same thing with {{CH3CH2OH}} and {{-SH}}. There is no reaction, because {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ol><p>One consequence is useful to remember. A hydroxyl group is a poor leaving group as it stands. It must first be converted into something that leaves more easily, for example by protonating the oxygen so that the group leaves as water, a far better leaving group. That is a different set of conditions from the one here.</p><p>The answer: hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, while chloride is a weak base and a good one. Methanol and chloride are much more stable than the reactants, so the equilibrium lies far to the right and chloride cannot displace hydroxide from methanol.</p>`,
  },
  // ---------- Passage 9 · In short
  8: {
    "1.1": `<p>In a <b>substitution</b>, one group takes the place of another on a carbon. In the kind called {{SN2}}, it happens in one step, and the carbon turns inside out, like an umbrella in the wind.</p>`,
    "1.2": `<p>In a <b>substitution</b>, one group takes the place of another group on a carbon. The group that comes in is the <b>nucleophile</b>. The group that goes out is the <b>leaving group</b>.</p><p>In the reaction in this chapter, it all happens in one step. The nucleophile comes in from the back, on the side opposite the leaving group. As it does, the carbon turns inside out, like an umbrella in the wind. This is called <b>inversion</b>.</p><p>It is called <b>{{SN2}}</b> because the speed depends on both starting molecules. The 2 stands for that.</p>`,
    "1.3": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> swaps one group for another on a carbon atom. A group called the <b>leaving group</b> goes out, and a new group called the <b>nucleophile</b> comes in. The nucleophile has a pair of electrons to share. The carbon has a small positive charge, so it welcomes them. The leaving group goes away with its pair of electrons.</p><p>Here are the main things to remember about the reaction called <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Speed.</strong> Take the nucleophile {{HS-}} and the molecule {{CH3I}}. Make either one twice as much, and the reaction goes twice as fast. The speed depends on both, so we say the reaction is <b>second order</b>. That is the 2 in {{SN2}}.</li><li><strong>One step.</strong> Everything happens at the same moment. There is one step and one hump on the energy diagram. There is no stop in the middle, so there is no <b>intermediate</b>. We call this <b>concerted</b>.</li><li><strong>From the back.</strong> The nucleophile comes in on the side opposite the leaving group. The carbon holds five groups for a moment, and the other three groups lie flat in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inside out.</strong> The carbon turns inside out, like an umbrella in the wind. This is <b>inversion</b>. A wedge in the drawing becomes a dash.</li><li><strong>One way only.</strong> The reaction of {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not run backward. Hydroxide is a strong base, and a strong base is a poor leaving group.</li></ul>`,
    "2.1": `<p>In an <b>{{SN2}}</b> reaction, a <b>nucleophile</b> pushes a <b>leaving group</b> off a carbon in one step, attacking from the back, so the carbon inverts.</p>`,
    "2.2": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on a carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>. These are the main points about <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Speed.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either one doubles the rate. We write rate = k[Nu][E]. The reaction is <b>second order</b>, and that is the 2 in {{SN2}}.</li><li><strong>One step.</strong> The reaction is <b>concerted</b>: one step, one <b>transition state</b>, one hump on the energy diagram, and no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>From the back.</strong> The nucleophile attacks from the back, 180° from the leaving group. In the transition state the carbon has five groups around it, and the other three lie in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> The carbon flips like an umbrella in the wind. One enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash.</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not run backward, because hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ul>`,
    "2.3": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> swaps a <b>leaving group</b> on a carbon for a <b>nucleophile</b>. The nucleophile is a Lewis base: it has an electron pair to give. The carbon compound it attacks is the <b>electrophile</b>, and its carbon has a small positive charge (δ+). The leaving group goes away with the pair of electrons that held it. Here is what to know about <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Speed.</strong> With {{HS-}} and {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate. So rate = k[Nu][E]. The reaction is <b>second order</b>, and that is the 2 in {{SN2}}.</li><li><strong>One step.</strong> The reaction is <b>concerted</b>. It has one step, one <b>transition state</b>, and one hump on the energy diagram. It has no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>From the back.</strong> The nucleophile comes in from the back, 180° from the leaving group. The transition state has five groups around the carbon, and the other three lie in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> The carbon turns inside out like an umbrella in the wind. One enantiomer gives one enantiomer: a wedge becomes a dash. This is called <b>Walden inversion</b>.</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not run backward, because hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ul>`,
    "3.1": `<p>An <b>{{SN2}}</b> reaction is a one-step, <b>concerted</b> <b>substitution</b> that is <b>second order</b>. The <b>nucleophile</b> attacks from the back, and the carbon <b>inverts</b>.</p>`,
    "3.2": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>, using the same two arrows as a proton transfer. Key points about <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the δ+ carbon compound. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair.</li><li><strong>Rate.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate: rate = k[Nu][E]. This is <b>second order</b>, which is the 2 in {{SN2}}.</li><li><strong>Concerted.</strong> One step, one <b>transition state</b>, one hump, no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>Back attack.</strong> The nucleophile attacks 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state with the other three groups in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> One enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash (<b>Walden inversion</b>).</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not reverse: hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ul>`,
    "3.3": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>. It uses the same two arrows as a proton transfer: one arrow from the electron pair of the nucleophile to the carbon, and one from the bond to the leaving group onto the leaving group. Here is what to remember about <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks the carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the compound with a δ+ carbon, which is what the nucleophile is drawn to. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair of electrons.</li><li><strong>Rate.</strong> Take {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}. Doubling either reactant doubles the rate, so rate = k[Nu][E]. The rate depends on two concentrations, so it is <b>second order</b>. That is the 2 in {{SN2}}, which stands for Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular. The step that sets the speed involves both molecules.</li><li><strong>Concerted.</strong> The reaction happens in one step. There is one <b>transition state</b> and one hump on the energy diagram. Nothing forms and then lives for a while in the middle, so there is no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>Back attack.</strong> The nucleophile attacks from the back, 180° from the leaving group. The transition state is <b>five-coordinate</b>: the carbon is partly bonded to both the nucleophile and the leaving group, and the other three groups lie in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> The carbon inverts. One enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash. This is <b>Walden inversion</b>.</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not run backward. Hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, so it cannot be pushed out by chloride.</li></ul>`,
    "4.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is a <b>concerted</b>, <b>second order</b> <b>substitution</b> in which the <b>nucleophile</b> attacks 180° from the <b>leaving group</b>, so the carbon <b>inverts</b>.</p>`,
    "4.2": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>, using the same two arrows as a proton transfer. Summary of <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the δ+ carbon compound. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair.</li><li><strong>Rate.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate: rate = k[Nu][E], <b>second order</b>, the 2 in {{SN2}}.</li><li><strong>Concerted.</strong> One step, one <b>transition state</b>, one hump on the energy diagram, no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>Back attack.</strong> The nucleophile attacks from the back, 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state with the other three groups in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> One enantiomer gives one enantiomer: a wedge becomes a dash (<b>Walden inversion</b>).</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not reverse, since hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ul>`,
    "4.3": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>, using the same two arrows as a proton transfer. The reasons behind each fact about <b>{{SN2}}</b> are as follows.</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the δ+ carbon compound, a Lewis acid at carbon. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair of electrons.</li><li><strong>Rate.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate: rate = k[Nu][E]. The rate depends on both reactants, so it is <b>second order</b> and the process is bimolecular. That is the 2 in {{SN2}}, from Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular. Both molecules are needed because both take part in the one step that sets the rate.</li><li><strong>Concerted.</strong> The bond to the nucleophile forms while the bond to the leaving group breaks. There is one step, one <b>transition state</b>, and one hump on the energy diagram. There is no <b>intermediate</b>, because there is only one step.</li><li><strong>Back attack.</strong> The nucleophile attacks from the back, 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state. The other three groups lie in one plane. In the transition state the nucleophile and leaving group are partly bonded on opposite sides of the carbon.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> The three other groups flip to the far side, like an umbrella in the wind. If the carbon is a stereocenter, one enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash. This is <b>Walden inversion</b>.</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not run backward. Hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, while chloride is a weak base and a good one, so the equilibrium lies far to the right.</li></ul>`,
    "5.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is a one-step, <b>concerted</b> <b>substitution</b> with a <b>five-coordinate</b> <b>transition state</b>, rate = k[Nu][E] (<b>second order</b>), in which back attack at 180° gives <b>inversion</b>.</p>`,
    "5.3": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>, using the same two arrows as a proton transfer. The points to know about <b>{{SN2}}</b>:</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the δ+ carbon compound. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair.</li><li><strong>Rate.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate: rate = k[Nu][E]. The reaction is <b>second order</b>, which is the 2 in {{SN2}} (Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular). Both reactants are in the one rate-determining step.</li><li><strong>Concerted.</strong> One step, one <b>transition state</b>, one hump on the energy diagram, no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>Back attack.</strong> The nucleophile attacks from the back, 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state, drawn [Nu···C···LG] ‡, with the other three groups in one plane.</li><li><strong>Inversion.</strong> The carbon inverts: one enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash (<b>Walden inversion</b>).</li><li><strong>No reverse.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not reverse, because hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group. Chloride is a weak base and a good leaving group, so the equilibrium lies far to the right.</li></ul>`,
    "6.1": `<p>{{SN2}} is a bimolecular, <b>concerted</b> <b>substitution</b> with rate = k[Nu][E] (<b>second order</b>) and a single <b>five-coordinate</b> <b>transition state</b>; backside attack at 180° to the <b>leaving group</b> gives <b>inversion</b> of configuration.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>, using the same two arrows as a proton transfer. In formal terms, <b>{{SN2}}</b> is characterized by the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the δ+ carbon compound, a Lewis acid at carbon. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair.</li><li><strong>Kinetics.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate: rate = k[Nu][E]. The rate law is <b>second order</b>, first order in each reactant, and the rate-determining step is bimolecular. That is the 2 in {{SN2}}.</li><li><strong>Concerted mechanism.</strong> Bond making and bond breaking occur in one step: one <b>transition state</b>, one hump on the energy diagram, no <b>intermediate</b>.</li><li><strong>Backside attack.</strong> The nucleophile approaches 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state, [Nu···C···LG] ‡. The other three groups lie in one plane.</li><li><strong>Stereochemistry.</strong> The carbon inverts: one enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash (<b>Walden inversion</b>).</li><li><strong>Reverse reaction.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} is effectively irreversible, because hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group.</li></ul>`,
    "6.3": `<p>A <b>substitution</b> replaces a <b>leaving group</b> on carbon with a <b>nucleophile</b>, using the same two arrows as a proton transfer. The <b>{{SN2}}</b> mechanism is defined by its kinetics, its transition state and its stereochemistry, and the three fit together.</p><ul><li><strong>Roles.</strong> The nucleophile is the Lewis base that attacks carbon. The <b>electrophile</b> is the δ+ carbon compound, a Lewis acid at carbon. The leaving group departs with the bonding pair of electrons.</li><li><strong>Kinetics.</strong> For {{HS-}} with {{CH3I}}, doubling either reactant doubles the rate: rate = k[Nu][E]. The rate law is <b>second order</b>, first order in each reactant. This matches a single rate-determining step in which both reactants take part, which is why the process is called bimolecular and why {{SN2}} carries a 2.</li><li><strong>Concerted mechanism.</strong> Bond formation to the nucleophile and bond cleavage to the leaving group are simultaneous. There is one <b>transition state</b> and one hump on the energy diagram, and no <b>intermediate</b>. A transition state is the maximum of the pathway, while an intermediate would be a minimum between two humps.</li><li><strong>Backside attack.</strong> The nucleophile approaches 180° from the leaving group, through a <b>five-coordinate</b> transition state, [Nu···C···LG] ‡, in which the carbon is partly bonded to both and the other three groups lie in one plane. Approach from the back keeps the nucleophile away from the leaving group and lets its electron pair interact with the antibonding orbital of the carbon-leaving group bond.</li><li><strong>Stereochemistry.</strong> The carbon inverts like an umbrella in the wind. One enantiomer gives one enantiomer, and a wedge becomes a dash. This is <b>Walden inversion</b>, the stereochemical signature of the mechanism.</li><li><strong>Leaving group ability.</strong> {{HO-}} with {{CH3Cl}} does not run in reverse. Hydroxide is a strong base and a poor leaving group, while chloride is a weak base and a good one, so the equilibrium lies far toward the products.</li></ul>`,
  },
};
